(1, 1, 1, 'approved', 'The Brass Lantern', 'The keeper’s house sat behind the lighthouse, where the ground sloped toward black rocks like the spine of some sleeping beast. The door was old, swollen with salt and age. Its keyhole was shaped like a teardrop.\r\n\r\nEdrin stood in the doorway while Harrow-Quill rummaged in a trunk. The elder’s hands moved with an odd reverence, as if he was afraid of waking something.\r\n\r\n“You think the lantern is just brass and glass,” Harrow-Quill murmured. “You think it’s only light.”\r\n\r\nHe held up a lantern.\r\n\r\nIt looked ordinary at first glance: tarnished brass, a handle worn smooth by years of use, clear glass panes framed by delicate etched spirals. But when Edrin leaned closer, he saw the etchings weren’t decoration. They formed tiny knot-work symbols that changed when the lantern shifted.\r\n\r\nAs if the lantern itself was trying to teach him how to read it.\r\n\r\n“The lantern is a contract,” Harrow-Quill said. “And contracts do not accept late payments.”\r\n\r\nEdrin’s mouth went dry. “Late payment to who?”\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill’s gaze flicked toward the lighthouse, its dark windowless face. “To the fog.”\r\n\r\nThat was not an answer. It was an omen dressed up as an explanation.\r\n\r\n“You’re too young,” Harrow-Quill continued, as if he’d heard the question anyway. “Young keepers hear too much.”\r\n\r\nEdrin thought of his mother’s hands, ink-stained and calm even when fear lived behind her eyes. He thought of his father walking out into mist and not walking back.\r\n\r\n“I’m old enough,” he said, surprised by his own certainty.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill studied him. Then, finally, he set the lantern into Edrin’s hands.\r\n\r\nIt was heavier than it looked. Not in metal-weight. In meaning-weight.\r\n\r\n“Rule one,” the elder said. “When the fog comes, you do not open doors you did not close. The village thinks the fog arrives. It does not. It is called.”\r\n\r\nEdrin stared at the lantern as if it might answer for itself.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill’s voice softened. “Rule two: if the lantern ever goes out completely, run. Don’t hide. Don’t bargain. Run.”\r\n\r\n“And rule three?” Edrin asked.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill hesitated, then said, “Rule three is the one no one survives learning.”\r\n\r\nHe stepped back. “You’ll start tonight.”', 'published', 0, '2026-05-09 12:20:43', '2026-05-23 13:17:09', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(2, 1, 2, 'approved', 'The Fog That Listened', 'That evening, Ashmoor grew strange.\r\n\r\nUsually the fog arrived in a slow drift, a soft curtain that made the harbor lights seem like small, embarrassed stars. This time it came like a tide swallowing footprints. It rolled in through alleys and over rooftops as if it had memorized the village’s map.\r\n\r\nPeople came to their doorways—tight-lipped, eyes narrowed—holding small charms made of saltwood and iron wire.\r\n\r\nEdrin walked the paths behind the lighthouse carrying the brass lantern. The fog curled around the lantern glass but did not smother it. The lantern light held steady, warm and amber, cutting a tunnel through the white like a memory you couldn’t erase.\r\n\r\nHe didn’t know how he knew this, but he understood immediately: the fog could smell the lantern.\r\n\r\nSomewhere down the slope, a bell rang once, a signal the village used only for emergencies.\r\n\r\nEdrin looked toward the keeper’s house, half expecting the fog to have filled it already.\r\n\r\nInstead, he saw a shape near the cliff markers.\r\n\r\nA person.\r\n\r\nOr something shaped like a person pretending to be one.\r\n\r\nThe figure stood too still, its outline sharpened against the fog like a drawing done with charcoal. When it moved, it didn’t step—its edges shifted, like the fog was deciding how much of it to show.\r\n\r\nEdrin lifted the lantern. The light struck the figure.\r\n\r\nFor a breath, the figure flinched—as if it had been touched by heat.\r\n\r\nThen it raised an arm.\r\n\r\nIt was wearing a glove made of something that looked like netting stretched into thin leather. The hand pointed toward Edrin’s chest, not at the lantern. As if it didn’t care about light. As if it cared about whatever light was attached to.\r\n\r\nEdrin swallowed.\r\n\r\n“What do you want?” he called.\r\n\r\nThe fog thickened.\r\n\r\nAnd the figure spoke.\r\n\r\nNot with a voice in his ears, but with a sound that formed behind his teeth. The words felt like they were being written directly onto his tongue.\r\n\r\n“Payment.”\r\n\r\nEdrin tightened his grip on the handle. “What payment?”\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s glass panes shimmered. The knot-work symbols on the brass flared faintly, as though remembering how to be alive.\r\n\r\nThe fog figure leaned closer. “What was taken must be returned.”\r\n\r\nEdrin’s mind raced. Taken? Returned? Everyone knew the lighthouse light was missing, but how did that connect to—\r\n\r\nThen he realized something he’d never allowed himself to think.\r\n\r\nIf the lighthouse was dark because the light was stolen… then someone had touched that lantern.\r\n\r\nSomeone had opened that contract.\r\n\r\nSomeone had made a choice.\r\n\r\nAnd maybe his father, Thane Vale, was part of it.\r\n\r\nEdrin remembered the last thing his father had said before walking out that night: “The wind is wrong. It smells like old iron.”\r\n\r\nThane had smelled the contract’s heartbeat.\r\n\r\nEdrin lifted the lantern higher.\r\n\r\n“What was taken,” he said carefully, “is not my choice.”\r\n\r\nThe figure’s outline blurred, as if it had to reassemble its anger.\r\n\r\n“All choices become payments.”\r\n\r\nThe bell in the village rang again, this time frantic.\r\n\r\nEdrin turned, suddenly aware of movement everywhere—the fog shifting as if it were searching for entrances. He could hear the distant sound of doors being slammed shut.\r\n\r\nAnd then he heard something else.\r\n\r\nA soft tapping on the lighthouse door.\r\n\r\nNot the keeper’s door behind the lighthouse.\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse door itself.\r\n\r\nEdrin hadn’t been near it. Not since he’d arrived. Not since Harrow-Quill had given him the lantern and told him to keep to the old rules.\r\n\r\nThe tapping came again.\r\n\r\nTap.\r\n\r\nTap.\r\n\r\nTap.\r\n\r\nLike a knuckle trying to find the right rhythm to pretend it belonged there.\r\n\r\nEdrin felt the lantern pull in his hands—like it had a gravity of its own.\r\n\r\nHe walked toward the lighthouse.\r\n\r\nAs he approached, the tapping stopped.\r\n\r\nThe fog leaned in, and the air smelled suddenly of salt and burning paper.\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse door had no handle on the outside.\r\n\r\nBut it did have a keyhole, teardrop-shaped like the keeper’s house.\r\n\r\nEdrin looked at the lantern in his hand.\r\n\r\nThe brass knot-work symbols rotated slowly, responding to the door like a compass needle responding to north.\r\n\r\nA thought—impossible, and yet clear as daylight—filled Edrin’s mind:\r\n\r\nThe lantern wasn’t just light.\r\n\r\nIt was the key.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-09 12:21:31', '2026-05-24 11:42:43', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(3, 1, 3, 'approved', 'A Door That Remembered Him', 'Edrin didn’t want to open the door.\r\n\r\nNot because he feared the dark; he’d lived in enough quiet houses with missing warmth to understand dark. He feared something more specific.\r\n\r\nHe feared that if he opened it, the lighthouse would show him what he didn’t want to know.\r\n\r\nBut his feet kept moving.\r\n\r\nHe held the lantern close to the door’s keyhole. The lantern glow deepened, turning amber into a richer gold. The glass panes brightened until the etched symbols became visible even through the fog’s veil.\r\n\r\nEdrin heard a faint click.\r\n\r\nThe keyhole flared with light.\r\n\r\nThe door—solid stone—began to move like it had been waiting for a long time to be asked.\r\n\r\nEdrin pulled it open.\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse interior was not a room.\r\n\r\nIt was a spiral of staircases wrapped around a void.\r\n\r\nThe air inside was colder than the outside fog, and it smelled like rain stored in a jar. The darkness wasn’t empty; it was textured, thick with the impression of many footsteps that had never finished landing.\r\n\r\nEdrin took a step inside.\r\n\r\nThe lantern light spread, illuminating carved markings on the walls. Not runes exactly—more like diagrams of feelings.\r\n\r\nPaths. Knots. Loops.\r\n\r\nAnd at the center of it all, above him, a platform where the lantern chamber should have been.\r\n\r\nBut instead of a lantern housing, there was a frame.\r\n\r\nA frame with empty hooks.\r\n\r\nAs if the lantern was missing a second half.\r\n\r\nEdrin looked down at his own lantern.\r\n\r\nHis lantern.\r\n\r\nNot the village’s dark lighthouse lantern.\r\n\r\nHis lantern.\r\n\r\nHe lifted it slightly, and the frame’s hooks responded by trembling.\r\n\r\nThen the air filled with whispers—not the fog-voice this time, but a chorus of quiet memories, like people speaking through walls.\r\n\r\nEdrin heard his mother laughing once, years ago, in a moment so tender it made his throat ache.\r\n\r\nHe heard his father humming, off-key, while repairing a net.\r\n\r\nHe heard Harrow-Quill’s voice saying, “Rule three is the one no one survives learning.”\r\n\r\nAnd he heard another voice, not belonging to anyone he knew.\r\n\r\nA voice like wind dragging across stone:\r\n\r\n“Keeper… return what was promised.”\r\n\r\nEdrin’s stomach clenched.\r\n\r\nHe had promised? He was a boy. A lantern carrier. A replacement for a vacancy.\r\n\r\nHe hadn’t signed anything.\r\n\r\nHad he?\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s glow intensified, and the knot-work symbols on its brass began to move, weaving into new shapes. Edrin’s eyes blurred as if the lantern was trying to show him a language he hadn’t studied.\r\n\r\nThen the meaning struck him in one sudden, painful clarity:\r\n\r\nThe lantern chose keepers the way storms chose ships.\r\n\r\nNot by consent.\r\n\r\nBy necessity.\r\n\r\nAshmoor had needed someone young enough not to understand the contract until it was too late.\r\n\r\nEdrin stepped back, but the stair spiral seemed to tighten. The lighthouse door behind him started to close on its own, slow and inevitable.\r\n\r\nTap tap tap—something outside.\r\n\r\nThe fog figure again.\r\n\r\nAs if it knew when Edrin would decide.\r\n\r\nEdrin grabbed the lantern with both hands and shouted, “If I give it what it wants, will it let Ashmoor go?”\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse air rippled.\r\n\r\nThe whisper chorus paused, as if waiting for the answer too.\r\n\r\nFinally, a voice answered—not from the fog, but from inside the lantern itself.\r\n\r\n“Release is not payment’s opposite.”\r\n\r\nEdrin felt anger rise, hot and sharp.\r\n\r\n“What is it, then?”\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s light pulsed once, like a heart acknowledging a question.\r\n\r\n“Release is payment’s delay.”\r\n\r\nThe door slammed shut behind him.\r\n\r\nNot entirely—he could still see a seam of fog-light at the edge—so he wasn’t trapped yet.\r\n\r\nBut he was running out of time.\r\n\r\nHe looked around at the empty hooks in the lantern chamber.\r\n\r\nA thought formed, terrible and clear:\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse wasn’t missing only light.\r\n\r\nIt was missing a person.\r\n\r\nA keeper who had become part of the contract.\r\n\r\nMaybe that was what had been taken.\r\n\r\nMaybe that was why the lighthouse never shone—because it had never been fully completed. Because the cost had been paid in blood or fear or choice—and the fog kept collecting interest.\r\n\r\nEdrin’s knees threatened to fold.\r\n\r\nThen his mother’s voice returned in his memory: Waiting is not the same thing.\r\n\r\nEdrin steadied himself.\r\n\r\nHe didn’t know how to fight a contract, but he knew something about repair.\r\n\r\nHis father had always said: “A thing breaks in patterns. If you learn the pattern, you can mend it.”\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s symbols spun faster. One shape landed into place inside Edrin’s mind like a key fitting a lock.\r\n\r\nPayment.\r\n\r\nPromise.\r\n\r\nReturn.\r\n\r\nDelay.\r\n\r\nHe turned toward the spiral wall where the carved diagrams formed a loop that ended in a single empty notch.\r\n\r\nA notch for an offering.\r\n\r\nAn offering that didn’t have to be blood—because blood was easy. Blood was what monsters asked for.\r\n\r\nBut contracts could be satisfied by other things too.\r\n\r\nBy truth.\r\nBy names.\r\nBy memory kept.\r\n\r\nEdrin stepped toward the notch and held the lantern out.\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s glow washed over the wall, and the diagram brightened.\r\n\r\nIn the center of the loop, faintly, a name formed in amber script—letters that weren’t carved into stone, but formed by light.\r\n\r\nThane Vale.\r\n\r\nEdrin gasped.\r\n\r\nIt wasn’t asking for a person he didn’t know.\r\n\r\nIt was asking for his father.\r\n\r\nEdrin’s heart hammered.\r\n\r\n“If you take him,” Edrin whispered, “you kill him.”\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse didn’t answer.\r\n\r\nThe fog outside tapped again, impatient, as if it could hear his fear.\r\n\r\nEdrin swallowed hard and looked down at his hands.\r\n\r\nThen he did something no one in Ashmoor had ever taught him to do.\r\n\r\nHe lied—not with words, but with a choice.\r\n\r\n“I return his name,” Edrin said firmly, voice trembling. “Not his life.”\r\n\r\nThe lantern flared.\r\n\r\nThe diagram shuddered.\r\n\r\nFor a moment, Edrin felt a tug in his chest as if the lighthouse was trying to pull a thread out of him and call it fate.\r\n\r\nHe held steady.\r\n\r\n“I return his name,” he repeated, “because he already paid enough by leaving.”\r\n\r\nThe air around him vibrated, furious.\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s glow dimmed sharply, then brightened again, as if struggling to interpret the contract’s rules.\r\n\r\nSomewhere beyond the lighthouse walls, the fog screamed—not a human sound, but a pressure release. The seam of fog-light at the lighthouse door pulsed.\r\n\r\nEdrin seized the moment.\r\n\r\nHe ran.\r\n\r\nUp the spiral, toward the empty frame where the lantern chamber should have held its light.\r\n\r\nHe reached the top platform and slammed the lantern into the frame’s hooks.\r\n\r\nThe hooks closed.\r\n\r\nThe lantern clicked in place like a puzzle piece being forced into perfection.\r\n\r\nA golden beam erupted downward, lighting the spiral stair as if sunrise had been trapped in the lighthouse for decades.\r\n\r\nAshmoor’s fog outside recoiled.\r\n\r\nEdrin could feel it like a shift in air pressure—the fog retreating from the door seam, shrinking like a predator losing interest.\r\n\r\nBut the contract wasn’t done.\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse air filled with whispers again, louder now, furious with the delay.\r\n\r\n“Keeper,” the voice said. “You have traded wrong.”\r\n\r\nEdrin’s vision swam. He leaned against the rail, breath coming hard.\r\n\r\n“What did I trade wrong?” he demanded.\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s light flickered, and Edrin realized something terrible.\r\n\r\nHe had returned a name.\r\n\r\nNot a person.\r\n\r\nBut the fog wanted balance. It wanted a life equivalent.\r\n\r\nIt would come for his father somehow. Somewhere. Later.\r\n\r\nContracts didn’t forgive; they postponed.\r\n\r\nRelease was delay.\r\n\r\nEdrin’s hands shook.\r\n\r\nHe stared into the beam of light and thought of his father walking out into mist. Thought of his mother’s calm fear, her careful ink lines, the way she tried to pretend missing people could be replaced by paperwork and prayers.\r\n\r\n“I can’t keep trading delays,” he whispered.\r\n\r\nThen the lantern—his lantern—answered him with warmth rather than cruelty.\r\n\r\nAs if it understood more than the contract voice did.\r\n\r\n“Then end the payment’s cycle.”\r\n\r\nEdrin stared at the empty frame.\r\n\r\n“How?” he asked, though his voice felt like it belonged to someone else.\r\n\r\nThe lantern’s brass symbols rearranged again.\r\n\r\nThis time, they formed a path that led downward, not upward.\r\n\r\nTo the sea.\r\nTo the harbor.\r\nTo the tide charts his mother handled.\r\nTo the record of who had been called and who had been taken.\r\n\r\nEdrin understood: the contract had been kept secret inside lighthouse mechanisms, inside village silence.\r\n\r\nBut contracts were written in stories as much as in stone.\r\n\r\nTo end a cycle, you didn’t only change the object.\r\n\r\nYou changed the story people believed.\r\n\r\nEdrin stepped away from the frame and headed for the stairs.\r\n\r\nThe beam followed him, illuminating the spiral until it looked less like a void and more like a tunnel through the past.\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse door seam widened as if something outside was losing its hold.\r\n\r\nEdrin ran down the', 'published', 0, '2026-05-09 12:22:09', '2026-05-24 14:20:34', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(4, 2, 1, 'approved', 'សំបុត្រនៅមុខទ្វារ', 'សៅភ្ញាក់ពីដំណេកហើយចេញទៅមើលមុខទ្វារ។ នៅទីនោះមានសំបុត្រចាស់មួយ ដាក់ត្រឹមជាន់ទ្វារ ដូចជារង់ចាំគាត់ជាយូរ។ នៅលើសំបុត្រមានឈ្មោះគាត់ ប៉ុន្តែគ្មានអាសយដ្ឋានអ្នកផ្ញើ។ គាត់ញញឹមបន្តិច ហើយបើកសំបុត្រយ៉ាងប្រុងប្រយ័ត្ន។', 'published', 0, '2026-05-09 12:24:43', '2026-05-24 14:12:21', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(5, 1, 4, 'approved', 'The Tide That Recorded Lies', 'Outside, the fog had receded enough for shapes to return: rooftops, paths, the cliffside line of markers. But the night still wasn’t safe. The fog didn’t vanish; it waited at the edges, listening.\r\n\r\nEdrin stumbled into the keeper’s house, almost tripping over the threshold.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill stood there, robe damp with mist, face pale.\r\n\r\n“You opened it,” the elder said.\r\n\r\nEdrin didn’t answer.\r\n\r\nInstead, he grabbed the trunk Harrow-Quill had been sorting through earlier and dragged out a stack of papers—maps, ledgers, old tide charts with ink that had faded into gray.\r\n\r\n“Where are the records?” Edrin demanded. “What was promised? Who paid?”\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill’s jaw tightened. “The village does not trade in names.”\r\n\r\nEdrin’s lantern burned steady behind him. The warmth filled the room like a living thing.\r\n\r\n“My father is in your contract,” Edrin said. “And my mother has been waiting for answers like she’s waiting for the tide.”\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill stared at the lantern beam with something close to fear.\r\n\r\n“You don’t understand,” the elder whispered. “If the village knows the true bargain, panic will—”\r\n\r\n“Panic is honest,” Edrin snapped. “Silence is what feeds the fog.”\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill’s shoulders slumped.\r\n\r\nFor a moment, he looked older than Edrin had ever imagined.\r\n\r\nThen he gestured toward the papers. “Read.”\r\n\r\nEdrin flipped through pages.\r\n\r\nThere were notes in tight handwriting—entries from years ago, when Ashmoor still believed in small mercies and quiet miracles. Tides were listed. Sailors’ returns were recorded. Storm dates were marked like wounds.\r\n\r\nAnd in the margin of many entries, in a symbol that matched the lantern’s knot-work, there was a phrase repeated:\r\n\r\nKeeper’s Debt.\r\n\r\nEdrin found a page with his father’s name.\r\n\r\nThane Vale.\r\nDate: two years ago.\r\nLocation: cliff markers at Ashmoor’s edge.\r\n\r\nUnder it, a note in Harrow-Quill’s hand—because Edrin recognized the sharp angles of the elder’s writing.\r\n\r\n“He offered the name to delay the taking.”\r\n\r\nEdrin stared until the letters blurred.\r\n\r\nHe hadn’t been taken.\r\n\r\nHe had been used.\r\n\r\nHe had volunteered because he believed he could buy time for his family, like trading a jewel for a day of daylight. Like a man stepping into mist to protect the people he loved.\r\n\r\nEdrin’s hands shook so badly the papers trembled.\r\n\r\n“So he’s still—” Edrin started.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill interrupted softly. “Not gone. Not yet.”\r\n\r\nEdrin looked up.\r\n\r\n“What does ‘not yet’ mean?”\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill didn’t meet his eyes.\r\n\r\n“It means the fog agreed to delay,” he said. “And delay still requires a collector.”\r\n\r\nEdrin realized the lantern light wasn’t only pushing back fog.\r\n\r\nIt was waking the contract.\r\n\r\nAnd contracts didn’t like being challenged.\r\n\r\nFootsteps sounded outside—quick, heavy.\r\n\r\nEdrin rushed to the door.\r\n\r\nHis mother stood there, hair pulled back, hands ink-smudged even in the night. Her eyes widened when she saw the lantern glow spilling from the doorway.\r\n\r\n“Mara,” Edrin breathed.\r\n\r\nShe stepped in slowly, scanning the room.\r\n\r\n“You look like you’ve seen a ghost,” she said.\r\n\r\nEdrin swallowed. “I saw the bargain.”\r\n\r\nMara’s expression didn’t change much, but her breath caught like a snapped string. She stared at the ledger in his hands.\r\n\r\nThen she whispered, “He offered his name, didn’t he.”\r\n\r\nEdrin nodded.\r\n\r\nMara’s face crumpled—not with collapse, but with release, like someone who had carried a weight so long they forgot where it started.\r\n\r\n“He left,” she said, voice breaking. “He left because I wouldn’t stop believing. I thought if I kept the tide charts, the sea would behave. If I kept my ink tidy, the world would keep its promises.”\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill stepped back, guilty. “Mara—”\r\n\r\nShe raised a trembling hand. “No. Don’t apologize. Help me finish what he started.”\r\n\r\nEdrin looked between them, confused.\r\n\r\n“What do you mean, finish?” he asked.\r\n\r\nMara took the ledger from his hands carefully, like it might cut her if she handled it wrong.\r\n\r\nThen she looked at the lantern beam and spoke a sentence so quiet it almost didn’t exist:\r\n\r\n“I return the promise to the sea.”\r\n\r\nEdrin shook his head. “That doesn’t sound like returning anything.”\r\n\r\nMara’s eyes were bright with unshed tears. “It does, if you understand the tide.”\r\n\r\nShe moved fast then—snatching a bowl, filling it with saltwater from the small basin near the back door. She pressed the ledger pages into her hands and began to smear the ink with wet fingers, erasing names and symbols.\r\n\r\nEdrin stared.\r\n\r\n“You can’t do that,” Harrow-Quill said.\r\n\r\nMara’s voice hardened. “The fog thrives on record. It uses memory like rope. If I break the record, I break the rope.”\r\n\r\nEdrin watched as she dragged her wet fingers across the margin symbol—once amber, now turning pale as the ink dissolved.\r\n\r\nThe lantern beam flickered.\r\n\r\nThe house groaned.\r\n\r\nThe air outside screamed again, but this time it sounded hurt rather than angry.\r\n\r\nEdrin understood what Mara was doing.\r\n\r\nShe wasn’t just changing a story.\r\n\r\nShe was denying the fog the contract’s language.\r\n\r\nA contract is only as strong as the belief that keeps it alive.\r\n\r\nThe lighthouse beam dimmed, then steadied—like a heartbeat choosing rhythm.\r\n\r\nMara finished erasing the last symbol on the page.\r\n\r\nIn the quiet that followed, Edrin heard something new.\r\n\r\nNot a whisper.\r\n\r\nA voice.\r\n\r\nHis father’s voice—faint as a far-off gull call, but unmistakably human.\r\n\r\n“Edrin?”\r\n\r\nEdrin turned toward the window.\r\n\r\nThe fog outside pulled back, revealing the cliffside path—wet stones glistening like freshly turned earth. Near the cliff markers, a silhouette moved.\r\n\r\nA man stumbled forward, soaked and shivering, as if he’d been held underwater by time itself.\r\n\r\nThane Vale.\r\n\r\nHe looked thinner than Edrin remembered. His hair clung to his forehead. His eyes were wide and alarmed, like someone waking from a long dream and finding the dream still watching.\r\n\r\nEdrin ran out into the fog-light and wrapped his arms around his father.\r\n\r\nThane made a sound between a laugh and a sob.\r\n\r\n“I offered a delay,” he rasped. “But I should have known. Contracts don’t end. They only change their payment.”\r\n\r\nEdrin pulled back, staring into his father’s face.\r\n\r\n“Then what did I change?” he asked.\r\n\r\nThane looked toward the lighthouse, now glowing steadily, no longer dim. No longer waiting.\r\n\r\n“You changed what we were willing to lose,” Thane said. “And what we refused to bargain away.”\r\n\r\nBehind them, Mara stood in the doorway, lantern light outlining her like warmth around a wound.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill whispered, almost reverently, “The lighthouse shines again.”\r\n\r\nBut the fog lingered at the edges, not gone entirely.\r\n\r\nBecause even if you break one contract, something else might be hungry for another.\r\n\r\nEdrin held his father’s hand and looked into the mist watching from afar.\r\n\r\n“Not yet,” he said, remembering the elder’s words.\r\n\r\nThen he added, louder, to the fog itself:\r\n\r\n“Not again.”', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 01:26:21', '2026-05-28 11:17:34', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(6, 1, 5, 'approved', 'The New Keeper’s Promise', 'Days passed.\r\n\r\nAshmoor’s lighthouse beam returned fully—steady, bright, undeniable. Sailors who’d avoided these waters for years began to steer by it again. Fishermen repaired their nets without flinching at sudden silence. Children laughed louder, as if joy could drown fear.\r\n\r\nBut Mara kept erasing the old records.\r\n\r\nNot everything—only the parts that fed the contract. She wrote new tide charts with fresh ink and new symbols, ones the fog didn’t recognize. She taught Edrin the difference between “remembering” and “recording,” between honoring a story and locking it into a cage.\r\n\r\nThane recovered slowly, waking some mornings with a blank look as if he were still deciding whether the world was real.\r\n\r\nHarrow-Quill did not return to his old certainty.\r\n\r\nHe walked the village at dusk now, lantern in hand, listening more than talking.\r\n\r\nAnd Edrin—\r\n\r\nEdrin became the keeper not because he wanted the job, but because he finally understood why people become keepers:\r\n\r\nNot to guard light.\r\n\r\nTo guard people from the price of believing in the wrong thing.\r\n\r\nThat night, when the fog rolled in again—because it always does, stubborn as winter—Edrin stood on the cliff markers with his brass lantern.\r\n\r\nHe watched the mist curl around the edges of the lighthouse beam like an animal circling a fire it couldn’t extinguish.\r\n\r\nThe fog did not retreat quickly.\r\n\r\nIt tested him, once.\r\n\r\nThe lantern responded by shining brighter, not in threat, but in refusal.\r\n\r\nEdrin lifted the lantern and spoke to the empty air like a promise made between human beings.\r\n\r\n“You don’t get to call payment forever,” he said.\r\n\r\nThe fog trembled.\r\n\r\nAnd somewhere in the distance, beyond the cliff, a bell rang—not frantic, but steady.\r\n\r\nNot an emergency signal.\r\n\r\nA greeting.\r\n\r\nAs if the world, at last, had remembered how to be awake.\r\n\r\nEdrin smiled.\r\n\r\nThen he walked back toward Ashmoor, lantern light carving a path through the mist—because this time, he wasn’t waiting for permission.\r\n\r\nHe was choosing.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 01:26:48', '2026-06-01 11:20:38', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(7, 2, 2, 'approved', 'ពាក្យដែលដាស់អតីតកាល', 'នៅខាងក្នុងមានក្រដាសមួយច្បាប់ ដែលសរសេរថា\r\n“បើអ្នកអានវា អ្នកអាចជួសជុលអ្វីមួយដែលខកខានកន្លងមក—ប៉ុន្តែត្រូវជ្រើសរើសដោយខ្លួនឯង។”\r\nសៅស្ទើរតែដួល ព្រោះគាត់ចាំបានថា កាលពីមុនគាត់ធ្លាប់ខកខានឱកាសមួយសម្រាប់អ្នកម្នាក់… ហើយគាត់ស្តាយរហូតមកដល់ថ្ងៃនេះ។', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 01:28:10', '2026-06-03 11:22:21', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(8, 3, 1, 'approved', 'ការតាមរកនៅចុងផ្លូវ', 'យប់នោះ សុវណ្ណរ័ត្នមិនអាចគេងលក់។ នាងបើកក្រដាសចាស់ដែលនាងបានឃើញក្នុងចិត្ត ហើយនាងសម្រេចចិត្តស្វែងរក “ចុងផ្លូវ”។\r\n\r\nផ្លូវនៅទីក្រុងចំរើនមានច្រើន។ តែក្នុងចិត្តនាង វាមានតែផ្លូវមួយដែលធ្វើឲ្យនាងមានអារម្មណ៍ពិសេស—ផ្លូវដែលនៅក្បែរបឹងតូច ហើយសល់សួនចាស់ដែលគេនិយាយថា នៅទីនោះមានរឿងអតីតកាលឆ្លងកាត់។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នដើរទៅទីនោះនៅម៉ោង ៨:៣០យប់។ ផ្លូវងងឹត ខ្យល់ត្រជាក់ ហើយសម្លេងសត្វល្អិតដូចជាកំពុងបន្លឺរឿងតូចៗ។ នាងឈប់នៅចុងផ្លូវ—កន្លែងដែលមានជញ្ជាំងចាស់ និងសញ្ញាខូចមួយលាបពណ៌ក្រហមស្លេក។\r\n\r\nនាងរកឃើញថា មានមនុស្សសរសេរអក្សរតូចមួយលើជញ្ជាំង៖\r\n“បើអ្នកស្វែងរកខ្ញុំ…មកតាមស្នាមញញឹម।”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នញញឹមមិនចេញ។ នាងមិនមែនជាមនុស្សសុបិនច្រើនទេ តែនៅពេលការពិតចាប់ផ្តើមជ្រាបចូលក្នុងជីវិត នាងទើបដឹងថា ចិត្តមនុស្សមិនអាចបិទទាំងស្រុងបាន។\r\n\r\nនាងចាំថា ស្នាមញញឹមលើក្រដាសចាស់មានបែបផែនពិសេស—ភ្នែកចំហៀងដូចជាកំពុងមើលចេញទៅទិសខាងស្ដាំ។ នាងឱនមើលជញ្ជាំង ហើយឃើញថា មានរូបគូរចំណុចតូចដូចជាដេគូរដោយដៃមនុស្ស។\r\n\r\nហើយនៅក្រោមចំណុចនោះ…មានប្រអប់តូចមួយកំពុងលាក់។\r\n\r\nនាងបើកប្រអប់។ ក្នុងនោះមានតែអ្វីមួយប៉ុណ្ណោះ—រូបថតខ្មៅស និងអក្សរមួយសន្លឹក។\r\n\r\nរូបថតនោះមានមនុស្សពីរនាក់។ មនុស្សម្នាក់គឺជាបុរសដែលនាងបានឃើញថ្ងៃនោះ។ មនុស្សម្នាក់ទៀត…គឺជាស្ត្រីវ័យក្មេងដែលមុខស្រដៀងទៅនឹងនាងយ៉ាងខ្លាំង។ សុវណ្ណរ័ត្នស្ទើរតែដួល។\r\n\r\nតើនាងជានរណា? ហើយតើរឿងនេះពាក់ព័ន្ធនឹងអតីតកាលរបស់នាងយ៉ាងដូចម្តេច?\r\n\r\nអក្សរនៅក្នុងសន្លឹកសរសេរថា៖\r\n“សុវណ្ណរ័ត្ន…ប្រសិនបើអ្នកបានរកឃើញរឿងនេះ មានន័យថា អ្នកមិនអាចបន្តរស់នៅដោយគ្មានចម្លើយទៀតបានទេ។ ខ្ញុំសុំទោសដែលមិនប្រាប់អ្នកឆាប់។ ខ្ញុំខ្លាចថា ការពិតនឹងធ្វើឲ្យអ្នកឈឺចាប់។ តែបើអ្នកត្រូវការចម្លើយ…ចូលទៅក្នុងបណ្ណាល័យចាស់នៅសង្កាត់ឆៀងខាងកើត នៅថ្ងៃស្អែកម៉ោង ៥។”\r\n\r\nហត្ថលេខានៅក្រោមអក្សរមានឈ្មោះមួយ៖ “គង់សុភា”។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នមិនស្គាល់ឈ្មោះនោះទេ។ តែពេលនាងមើលរូបថតម្តងទៀត នាងយល់ថា ខ្លួនឯងមិនបានភ្លេចអតីតកាលទេ—អតីតកាលគ្រាន់តែបិទមាត់ ដើម្បីរង់ចាំពេលត្រឹមត្រូវ។', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 01:34:18', '2026-05-24 14:12:52', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(9, 3, 2, 'approved', 'បណ្ណាល័យចាស់ និងចម្លើយដែលឈឺ', 'ថ្ងៃស្អែក ម៉ោង៥ល្ងាច សុវណ្ណរ័ត្នទៅបណ្ណាល័យចាស់។ វាជាអាគារដែលមើលទៅដូចជាត្រូវបោះបង់ តែខាងក្នុងនៅតែមានសៀវភៅជាច្រើន និងធូលីដែលរ៉ាប់រងការចងចាំ។\r\n\r\nនៅទីនោះ នាងជួបបុរសម្នាក់—គង់សុភា។ គាត់ពាក់អាវដូចថ្ងៃមុន ហើយភ្នែកគាត់មិនបានផ្លាស់ប្តូរច្រើនទេ។ គាត់មើលនាងដូចជាមនុស្សដែលបានរង់ចាំយូរ ហើយពេលដែលជួប ក៏មិនដឹងថាត្រូវនិយាយអ្វីដំបូង។\r\n\r\n“អ្នក…អ្នកមកហើយ” គាត់និយាយ។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នចង្អុលរូបថតក្នុងកាបូប។\r\n“តើនេះជាអ្វី? ហេតុអ្វីមុខខ្ញុំស្រដៀងស្ត្រីក្នុងរូប?”\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា ដកដង្ហើមធ្ងន់។\r\n“ព្រោះនាងជាម្ដាយបង្កើតរបស់អ្នក…ប៉ុន្តែខ្ញុំមិនគួរនិយាយថា ‘បង្កើត’ ដោយស្រួលទេ។ មានរឿងច្រើន។ មានការសម្រេចចិត្តដែលយើងធ្វើកាលពីអតីតកាល…ហើយសម្រេចចិត្តទាំងនោះ វាយប្រហារយ៉ាងស្ងៀមស្ងាត់។”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នមិនសម្រួលអារម្មណ៍។ នាងខឹង។ នាងភ្ញាក់ផ្អើល។ នាងទាំងសង្ស័យ និងស្រួយចិត្ត។\r\n\r\n“អតីតកាលរបស់ខ្ញុំ…តើអ្នកលាក់អ្វី?”\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា បើកប្រអប់ឯកសារតូចមួយ ហើយយកចេញនូវឯកសារចាស់ៗ។ គាត់បង្ហាញថា នៅពេលនាងនៅក្មេង ស្ថានភាពគ្រួសារមានវិបត្តិ។ មនុស្សម្នាក់បានជួយនាងឲ្យរស់នៅ មុនពេលមនុស្សពាក់ព័ន្ធបាត់ខ្លួន។ គាត់—គង់សុភា—ជាអ្នកដែលនៅជិតពេលនោះ ហើយគាត់បានចូលរួមក្នុងការការពារ។ តែការការពារនោះ…បានធ្វើឲ្យនាងមិនដឹងសេចក្ដីពិតអំពីឪពុកម្តាយខ្លួន។\r\n\r\n“ខ្ញុំមិនចង់ឲ្យអ្នកដឹងពេលអ្នកនៅតូច។ ព្រោះខ្ញុំខ្លាច…អ្នកនឹងបែកបាក់។ តែឥឡូវ…អ្នកធំហើយ។ អ្នកអាចទ្រាំជាមួយការពិតបាន។”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នដាក់ទដៃលើថ្ងាស។ នាងមានអារម្មណ៍ដូចជាផែនដីរលំ ប៉ុន្តែដូចជាភ្លាមៗក៏មានពន្លឺតិចៗនៅក្នុងចិត្ត—ព្រោះយ៉ាងហោចណាស់ ចម្លើយកំពុងមក។\r\n\r\n“ហើយស្នាមញញឹម? នៅលើក្រដាសចាស់…ហេតុអ្វីវាមកជាប់ខ្ញុំ?”\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា ញញឹមយ៉ាងសោកស្តាយ។\r\n“ស្នាមញញឹមនោះ…ជារបស់នាង។ ម្ដាយបង្កើតអ្នក។ នៅថ្ងៃដែលគាត់ចាកចេញ គាត់ទុកស្នាមញញឹមនេះ។ គាត់ចង់ឲ្យអ្នកមិនភ័យ។ គាត់ចង់ឲ្យអ្នកចងចាំថា សូម្បីតែថ្ងៃខ្មៅ…យើងនៅតែអាចរកសេចក្ដីល្អបាន។”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នឈឺចាប់។ តែនាងក៏មានចិត្តពេញដោយភាពរឹងមាំដែលនាងមិនធ្លាប់គិតថាមាន។\r\n“បើម្ដាយខ្ញុំចង់ឲ្យខ្ញុំមិនខ្លាច…តើអ្នកធ្វើឲ្យខ្ញុំខ្លាចដូចម្តេច?”\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា ធ្លាក់សម្លេង។\r\n“ខ្ញុំសុំទោស…ខ្ញុំខ្លាចថា អ្នកនឹងជឿខ្ញុំមិនបាន។ ខ្ញុំខ្លាចថា អ្នកនឹងស្អប់ខ្ញុំ។ តែខ្ញុំក៏មិនចង់ឲ្យអ្នកទ្រាំដោយគ្មានដឹងពីការពិត។”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នមើលមុខគាត់យូរ។ នៅពេលនាងនិយាយចុងក្រោយ គឺជាសម្លេងទន់ជាងមុន៖\r\n“ខ្ញុំមិនដឹងថាខ្ញុំគួរជឿអ្នកទេឬទេ…ប៉ុន្តែខ្ញុំយល់ថា អ្នកក៏រងទុក្ខដូចគ្នា។”\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា ភ្ញាក់ផ្អើល។\r\n“អ្នក…អត់ទោសខ្ញុំទេ?”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នមើលទៅក្រៅបង្អួច។ ពន្លឺចុងរសៀលធ្លាក់លើធូលី។\r\n“ខ្ញុំមិនទាន់អាចអត់ទោសបានភ្លាមៗ។ ប៉ុន្តែខ្ញុំអាចចាប់ផ្តើមស្វែងរកផ្លូវថ្មី។ ហើយស្នាមញញឹម…ខ្ញុំចង់ឲ្យវាមានន័យថា ខ្ញុំមិនមែននៅតែជាមនុស្សដែលរង់ចាំអតីតកាលនោះទេ។ ខ្ញុំចង់រស់នៅក្នុងថ្ងៃនេះ។”', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 01:34:40', '2026-05-26 10:02:32', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(10, 3, 3, 'approved', 'ក្តីស្នេហាដែលមិនបានសុំឈ្មោះ', 'សុវណ្ណរ័ត្ននៅតែធ្វើការដូចមុន។ តែបន្ទាប់ពីថ្ងៃនោះ នាងមិនដូចមុនទេ។ នាងចាប់ផ្តើមសួររឿងអំពីមនុស្សជុំវិញខ្លួន—មនុស្សដែលជួយនាងពេលនាងនៅក្មេង អ្នកជិតខាងចាស់ៗ អ្នករក្សាទុកឯកសារនៅសាលារៀនដែលនាងធ្លាប់ទៅ។\r\n\r\nប៉ុន្តែគង់សុភា មិនបានសុំឲ្យនាងត្រូវការគាត់ភ្លាមៗទេ។ គាត់នៅជិត តែគាត់មិនបង្ខំ។ គាត់គ្រាន់តែបើកឲ្យសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នជ្រើសរើសផ្លូវដោយខ្លួនឯង។\r\n\r\nពេលសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នរៀបចំឯកសារតែម្តងនៅសាលាមេត្រី នាងក៏បានជួបមនុស្សម្នាក់ផ្សេងទៀត—បុរសវ័យក្មេងម្នាក់ឈ្មោះ “រតនា” ដែលជាអ្នកជំនួយការបច្ចេកទេស។ គាត់ជាមនុស្សសប្បាយ តែស្នេហារបស់គាត់មិនបានមកជាអំពើឆាប់រហ័សទេ។ គាត់គោរពព្រំដែននាង ហើយសួរតែពេលដែលនាងអាចឆ្លើយបាន។\r\n\r\nថ្ងៃមួយ រតនា បាននិយាយ៖\r\n“បងស្រី…អ្នកមើលទៅដូចជាមនុស្សដែលទើបចេញពីព្យុះ។ តែអ្នកនៅតែឈរ។ ខ្ញុំចូលចិត្តការឈររបស់អ្នក។”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នញញឹមតិចៗ។\r\n“ខ្ញុំមិនដឹងថាខ្ញុំឈរដោយកម្លាំងរបស់អ្នកណាទេ។ តែខ្ញុំគិតថា…ខ្ញុំឈរដោយសេចក្ដីសម្រេចចិត្ត។”\r\n\r\nរតនា ស្ងៀមមួយភ្លែត។\r\n“បើអ្នកត្រូវការនរណាម្នាក់ដើរជាមួយ…ខ្ញុំនៅទីនេះ។ ខ្ញុំមិនចង់ឲ្យអ្នកយកអតីតកាលជាខ្សែសង្ស័យឲ្យខ្លួនឯងទៀតទេ។”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នគិតដល់គង់សុភា។ នាងមិនចង់បែកបាក់អារម្មណ៍បុរសណាម្នាក់។ តែការពិតគឺថា…ការពិតកំពុងបង្រៀននាងឲ្យមើលសេចក្ដីល្អនៅមនុស្សគ្រប់រូប។', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 02:22:38', '2026-06-03 11:44:03', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(11, 3, 4, 'approved', 'សេចក្ដីស្នេហាដែលបំបាត់ការភ័យ', 'នៅពេលអស់សប្តាហ៍ នាងទទួលដំណឹងថា អ្នកដែលពាក់ព័ន្ធនឹងអតីតកាលម្ដាយបង្កើតនាង ត្រូវបានគេឃើញនៅខេត្តឆ្ងាយ។ មនុស្សមួយចំនួននៅតែរក្សាការសម្ងាត់។ ហើយគង់សុភា ទទួលមូលហេតុឲ្យតាមស្រាវជ្រាវបន្ត។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នសម្រេចចិត្តទៅជាមួយគាត់។ នាងមិនចង់ឲ្យការពិតបន្តលាក់។ នាងចង់ឲ្យការពិតបានបញ្ចប់—ដោយមិនមែនបញ្ចប់ដោយការឈឺចាប់ទេ ប៉ុន្តែបញ្ចប់ដោយការយល់ដឹង។\r\n\r\nពេលទៅដល់កន្លែងនោះ មានមនុស្សចាស់ម្នាក់បាននិយាយថា ម្ដាយបង្កើតរបស់នាង គឺជាមនុស្សដែលជួយក្មេងជាច្រើន ហើយគាត់ធ្លាប់សន្យាថា គាត់នឹងទុកស្នាមញញឹមមួយសម្រាប់ថ្ងៃដែលកូនគាត់ធំ។ តែគាត់ក៏ធ្លាប់បាត់ខ្លួន ដោយសារបញ្ហាគ្រួសារនៅសម័យមុន។\r\n\r\nពេលនាងឮដូច្នោះ នាងមិនបានខឹងតែប៉ុណ្ណោះទេ។ នាងក៏មានសំណើចតិចៗក្នុងទឹកភ្នែក។ ព្រោះស្នេហារបស់ម្ដាយនាង…មិនបានបាត់ទេ។ វាគ្រាន់តែបានផ្លាស់ទម្រង់ទៅជាការរង់ចាំ និងការបញ្ជូនសញ្ញា។\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា ដួលអង្គុយពេលឮព័ត៌មាន។ គាត់យំយ៉ាងស្ងៀមៗ—ហាក់ដូចជាកំពុងទទួលអ្វីមួយដែលគាត់បានខ្វះអស់រយៈពេលយូរ។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នឈរជិតគាត់ ហើយនិយាយថា៖\r\n“ខ្ញុំមិនចង់ឲ្យអ្នកនៅត្រូវទោសខ្លួនឯងទៀតទេ។ បើអ្នកស្រឡាញ់នាង…សូមឲ្យការស្រឡាញ់នោះទៅជាកម្លាំងល្អ។”\r\n\r\nគង់សុភា ប្រែភ្នែក។\r\n“អ្នកនិយាយដូចជាម្ដាយអ្នកមែនទែន…”\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នញញឹម—ជាស្នាមញញឹមដែលខ្លួននាងទើបរកឃើញ។\r\n“មិនមែនដូចគាត់ទេ។ ខ្ញុំគ្រាន់តែជាកូនគាត់។ ការស្រឡាញ់មួយ…មិនដែលរលាយទេ។ វាគ្រាន់តែប្តូរពីការរស់នៅក្នុងអតីតកាល ទៅជាការរស់នៅក្នុងថ្ងៃនេះ។”', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 02:22:56', '2026-06-03 11:44:22', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(12, 3, 5, 'approved', 'ចុងផ្លូវដែលមិនមែនជាទីបញ្ចប់', 'ត្រឡប់មកទីក្រុងវិញ នៅយប់មួយ សុវណ្ណរ័ត្នដើរតាមផ្លូវដដែល—ផ្លូវដែលនាំនាងទៅកាន់ប្រអប់សម្ងាត់កាលពីដំបូង។ នាងមិនទៅដើម្បីស្វែងរកអ្វីថ្មីទៀតទេ។ នាងទៅដើម្បីបិទរឿងមួយក្នុងចិត្ត។\r\n\r\nនាងយកក្រដាសចាស់ដែលមានស្នាមញញឹម ហើយគូរតាមស្នាមនោះ ដោយប្រើពណ៌ខ្មៅដៃបន្តិច។ បន្ទាប់មក នាងសរសេរអក្សរបន្ថែមនៅក្រោមស្នាមញញឹម៖\r\n\r\n“អតីតកាលអាចពិបាក។ តែខ្ញុំមិនពិបាកក្នុងការរស់ទេ។ ខ្ញុំជ្រើសរើសឲ្យថ្ងៃនេះមានអត្ថន័យ។”\r\n\r\nពេលនាងបញ្ចប់ នាងឮសំឡេងដើរនៅពីក្រោយ។ គង់សុភា ឈរនៅទីនោះ។ គាត់មិននិយាយច្រើន។ គាត់គ្រាន់តែសម្លឹងមើលស្នាមញញឹមលើក្រដាស ហើយញញឹម—ញញឹមដែលចុងក្រោយមើលទៅបែបស្រាលជាងមុន។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នក៏ឃើញរតនា ពីក្រោយដែរ។ គាត់មកតាមពេលឮនាងមិននៅផ្ទះ។ គាត់មិនសួរច្រើន។ គាត់គ្រាន់តែដាក់ដៃលើស្មា ស្រាលៗ ដូចជាប្រាប់ថា “អ្នកមិននៅតែតែម្នាក់ឯងទេ”។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នបិទភ្នែកមួយភ្លែត។ នាងដឹងថា “ចុងផ្លូវ” មិនមែនជាទីបញ្ចប់។ វាគ្រាន់តែជាចំណុចដែលយើងឈប់មើលទៅក្រោយ ហើយចាប់ផ្តើមមើលទៅមុខ។\r\n\r\nនៅពេលព្រលប់ប្រែទៅជារាត្រី ផ្ទៃមេឃពណ៌ខ្មៅស្រអាប់ ប៉ុន្តែស្នាមញញឹមនៅក្នុងដៃនាងនៅតែភ្លឺ។ ហើយពន្លឺនោះ មិនមែនជាអំណាចមន្តអាគមទេ—វាជាភស្តុតាងថា មនុស្សម្នាក់អាចឆ្លងកាត់អ្វីៗ ហើយនៅតែរកឃើញខ្លួនឯងបាន។\r\n\r\nសុវណ្ណរ័ត្នបានដើរចេញពីទីនោះ ជាមួយនឹងជំហានថ្មី។ ជាមួយនឹងក្តីសង្ឃឹមថ្មី។\r\nហើយនៅក្នុងចិត្តនាង នាងដឹងថា ស្នេហាដ៏ពិត…គឺមិនមែនសម្រាប់ការត្រឡប់ទៅអតីតកាលទេ។ វាសម្រាប់ការរស់នៅ—ដោយមិនខ្លាច—ជារៀងរហូត។', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 02:23:12', '2026-06-03 11:58:11', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(13, 5, 1, 'approved', 'The Boy Who Smiled Like Summer', 'The first time Sang Zhi saw Duan Jiaxu, she was thirteen years old and hiding behind her brother’s bedroom door.\r\n\r\nThe room smelled like instant noodles and old books. Her brother, Sang Yan, was loudly complaining while a tall boy beside him leaned back lazily in the chair, laughing as if nothing in the world could bother him.\r\n\r\n“Your sister is spying again,” Duan Jiaxu suddenly said.\r\n\r\nSang Zhi froze.\r\n\r\nSang Yan turned around immediately. “Sang Zhi! Stop acting weird and go back to your room!”\r\n\r\nEmbarrassed, she was about to run when another voice stopped her.\r\n\r\n“Wait.”\r\n\r\nDuan Jiaxu walked toward her and held out a small strawberry candy. His eyes curved gently when he smiled.\r\n\r\n“For you, little kid.”\r\n\r\nSang Zhi stared at the candy in her palm long after he left that night.\r\n\r\nShe didn’t know it yet, but her first love had already begun.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 03:04:06', '2026-05-24 11:21:13', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(14, 5, 2, 'approved', 'Secretly Watching Him', 'After that day, Duan Jiaxu started visiting their house often.\r\n\r\nSometimes he came to study with Sang Yan. Sometimes he stayed for dinner because it was too late to go home. Every time he appeared, Sang Zhi pretended not to care.\r\n\r\nBut secretly, she noticed everything.\r\n\r\nThe way he always thanked her mother after meals.\r\n\r\nThe way he tapped her head lightly whenever she looked upset.\r\n\r\nThe way he smiled even when he seemed tired.\r\n\r\nOne rainy evening, the electricity suddenly went out.\r\n\r\nSang Zhi hated thunder. She sat alone in the living room, hugging her knees while lightning flashed outside the windows.\r\n\r\nThen a warm flashlight appeared beside her.\r\n\r\n“You’re scared?” Duan Jiaxu asked softly.\r\n\r\n“I’m not.”\r\n\r\nAnother thunderclap sounded immediately.\r\n\r\nShe nearly jumped.\r\n\r\nDuan Jiaxu laughed quietly before sitting beside her on the sofa.\r\n\r\n“Alright,” he said gently. “Then I’ll stay here until the rain stops.”\r\n\r\nThat night, Sang Zhi realized something dangerous.\r\n\r\nThe more she looked at him, the harder it became to look away.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 03:18:58', '2026-06-01 11:19:18', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(15, 5, 3, 'approved', 'The Feeling Called First Love', 'Middle school became unbearable after Sang Zhi discovered what liking someone truly meant.\r\n\r\nEvery little thing reminded her of Duan Jiaxu.\r\n\r\nA basketball court.\r\n\r\nMint candy.\r\n\r\nLate-night city lights.\r\n\r\nEven hearing someone mention university students made her heart react strangely.\r\n\r\nOne afternoon, Sang Zhi waited outside her school gate and saw Duan Jiaxu standing there beside his motorcycle.\r\n\r\n“You skipped class?” he asked.\r\n\r\n“I didn’t.”\r\n\r\n“You look guilty.”\r\n\r\n“I said I didn’t!”\r\n\r\nHe laughed and handed her a warm milk tea.\r\n\r\n“Your brother asked me to pick you up.”\r\n\r\nSang Zhi lowered her head quietly so he wouldn’t see her smiling.\r\n\r\nThe sunset painted the streets orange as she sat behind him on the motorcycle ride home.\r\n\r\nShe carefully grabbed the corner of his jacket.\r\n\r\nJust a tiny piece.\r\n\r\nBut even that small touch made her heart race wildly.\r\n\r\nAt fifteen years old, Sang Zhi finally understood why people called first love both sweet and painful.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 06:33:25', '2026-06-01 11:19:22', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(16, 5, 4, 'approved', 'Distance', 'As time passed, Duan Jiaxu stopped coming over as often.\r\n\r\nUniversity became busier.\r\n\r\nLife became heavier.\r\n\r\nSometimes Sang Zhi overheard adults whispering about his family problems and financial struggles. She wanted to ask if he was okay, but every time she saw him, he still smiled the same way.\r\n\r\nAs if nothing could hurt him.\r\n\r\nOne winter night, Sang Zhi sent him a message.\r\n\r\n“Are you busy lately?”\r\n\r\nSeveral minutes passed before the reply arrived.\r\n\r\n“Mm. A little.”\r\n\r\nThat was all.\r\n\r\nSang Zhi stared at the short message until the phone screen dimmed.\r\n\r\nFor the first time, she realized growing up also meant drifting apart from people you cared about.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 06:37:34', '2026-06-01 11:19:26', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(17, 5, 5, 'approved', 'Meeting Again', 'Years later, Sang Zhi moved to another city for university.\r\n\r\nThe city was unfamiliar, crowded, and cold.\r\n\r\nOne evening, heavy rain trapped her outside a convenience store. She stood under the small roof, frustrated, until someone opened an umbrella beside her.\r\n\r\n“Still afraid of rain?”\r\n\r\nHer body froze instantly.\r\n\r\nDuan Jiaxu stood there wearing a dark coat, taller and more mature than before.\r\n\r\nBut his smile was exactly the same.\r\n\r\nSang Zhi suddenly felt like she had returned to being thirteen years old again.\r\n\r\n“…Long time no see,” she whispered.\r\n\r\nThe rain continued falling around them while the city lights blurred softly in the distance.\r\n\r\nNeither of them realized it then.\r\n\r\nBut this reunion would change both of their lives forever.', 'published', 0, '2026-05-10 06:38:06', '2026-06-01 11:19:29', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
