(42, 21, 1, 'approved', 'The First Glance', 'The summer air was warm, filled with the quiet buzz of a new school year about to begin.\r\n\r\nSu Zaizai stood at the school gate, clutching her bag tightly, her eyes wandering with curiosity. Everything felt new—new uniforms, new faces, new possibilities.\r\n\r\nAnd then she saw him.\r\n\r\nA boy standing quietly under a tree, his posture straight, his expression calm—almost distant. While everyone else seemed nervous or excited, he looked… untouched by it all.\r\n\r\n“Who is that?” she whispered to herself.\r\n\r\nThat was the first time Su Zaizai saw Zhang Lurang.\r\n\r\nAnd somehow, her world shifted.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-01 10:31:48', '2026-06-01 12:56:58', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(43, 21, 2, 'approved', 'Sitting One Row Apart', 'Fate worked in small, quiet ways.\r\n\r\nZaizai didn’t end up sitting next to Zhang Lurang—but close enough. One row apart. Just enough for her to steal glances during class.\r\n\r\nHe was always focused. Always silent. Always first to finish his work.\r\n\r\n“Top student,” someone whispered behind her.\r\n\r\nOf course he was.\r\n\r\nMeanwhile, Zaizai was… average. Cheerful, talkative, and a little clumsy.\r\n\r\nBut that didn’t stop her.\r\n\r\n“Hi! I’m Su Zaizai,” she said one day, leaning toward him with a bright smile.\r\n\r\nZhang Lurang paused, slightly surprised.\r\n“…Zhang Lurang.”\r\n\r\nThat was it. Short. Simple.\r\n\r\nBut to her, it was everything.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-01 10:32:04', '2026-06-08 13:43:14', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(44, 21, 3, 'approved', 'The Beginning of “Us”', 'Friendships began forming like constellations.\r\n\r\nZaizai gathered people around her naturally—her laughter was contagious. Soon, a small group formed: lively, supportive, inseparable.\r\n\r\nAnd somehow… Zhang Lurang became part of it.\r\n\r\nHe didn’t talk much. But he stayed.\r\n\r\nZaizai noticed the small things:\r\n\r\nHe always listened\r\nHe helped quietly\r\nHe remembered details others forgot\r\n\r\n“Lurang, you’re actually really nice,” she said one afternoon.\r\n\r\nHe looked at her, slightly confused.\r\n\r\nNo one had said that to him before.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-01 10:32:18', '2026-06-08 13:51:32', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(45, 21, 4, 'pending', 'The Quiet Boy’s World', 'Behind Zhang Lurang’s calm exterior was a heavy silence.\r\n\r\nExpectations. Pressure. Comparisons.\r\n\r\nHe was always “the good son,” “the perfect student,” “the one who shouldn’t fail.”\r\n\r\nBut never just… himself.\r\n\r\nZaizai didn’t fully understand it at first.\r\n\r\nShe just knew one thing—\r\n\r\nHe looked lonely.\r\n\r\nSo she stayed.\r\n\r\nShe talked more. Laughed more. Pulled him into conversations, into sunlight, into moments he never allowed himself to have.\r\n\r\nAnd slowly…\r\n\r\nHe began to change.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-01 10:39:51', '2026-06-21 12:02:55', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(46, 23, 1, 'approved', 'The Rain That Started It All', 'The rain came suddenly that afternoon, heavy and restless, drumming against the classroom windows like it had something urgent to say.\r\n\r\nInside, the air was thick with noise—students laughing, chairs scraping, the low hum of conversations filling every corner. It was the kind of ordinary chaos that Xu Sui had grown used to, the kind she quietly blended into without ever standing out.\r\n\r\nShe sat by the window, as always.\r\n\r\nHer fingers rested lightly on the edge of her notebook, a pen tucked neatly between them. The pages were filled with careful handwriting—every stroke deliberate, every word aligned as if order could bring her a sense of control over everything else in her life.\r\n\r\nOutside, the sky was gray.\r\n\r\nInside, she was invisible.\r\n\r\nXu Sui didn’t mind.\r\n\r\nShe had never been the type to seek attention. Being unnoticed meant being safe. No expectations, no disappointments. Just a quiet rhythm of school, home, and the small, predictable world she understood.\r\n\r\nBut that day—\r\nsomething changed.\r\n\r\nThe classroom door slid open with a sharp sound, letting in a gust of cool air mixed with the scent of rain. A few students turned to look, their conversations briefly interrupted.\r\n\r\nXu Sui didn’t.\r\n\r\nNot at first.\r\n\r\nIt wasn’t until she heard laughter—clear, unrestrained, and completely out of place in the dull gray afternoon—that she lifted her head.\r\n\r\nAnd then she saw him.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze stood at the doorway, one hand casually gripping the strap of his backpack. His uniform shirt clung slightly to his shoulders, damp from the rain, and his hair—dark and messy—still held droplets that caught the light.\r\n\r\nHe didn’t seem bothered.\r\n\r\nIf anything, he looked… alive.\r\n\r\nLike the storm outside belonged to him.\r\n\r\n“Hey, you’re late again,” someone called from the back.\r\n\r\n“So what?” he replied easily, stepping into the room. “The rain slowed me down.”\r\n\r\n“That’s just an excuse!”\r\n\r\nHe laughed.\r\n“Then I’ll use it anyway.”\r\n\r\nThe class erupted into amusement.\r\n\r\nXu Sui watched silently.\r\n\r\nThere was something about him—something she couldn’t quite explain. Maybe it was the way he spoke without hesitation, or how he moved through the room like he had nothing to prove to anyone.\r\n\r\nHe didn’t try to fit in.\r\n\r\nHe simply existed—and somehow, the world adjusted around him.\r\n\r\nHer gaze lingered longer than it should have.\r\n\r\nWhen he walked past her row, she quickly lowered her head, pretending to focus on her notebook. Her pen hovered over the page, unmoving.\r\n\r\nHer heart, however, refused to stay still.\r\n\r\nIt beat faster—louder—like it was trying to draw attention to something she didn’t yet understand.\r\n\r\nShe pressed her lips together, confused.\r\n\r\nWhy?\r\n\r\nShe had seen many classmates before. People laughed, talked, lived their lives every day around her. Nothing about it had ever affected her like this.\r\n\r\nBut this time…\r\nit was different.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze dropped into his seat near the back, leaning back in his chair as if the rules of the classroom didn’t apply to him. A few girls nearby immediately started talking to him, their voices softer, more eager.\r\n\r\nHe responded casually, sometimes teasing, sometimes ignoring them altogether.\r\n\r\nIt didn’t seem to matter.\r\n\r\nThey still smiled.\r\n\r\nXu Sui looked away.\r\n\r\nShe told herself it wasn’t important.\r\n\r\nJust another classmate.\r\nJust another ordinary day.\r\n\r\nBut her mind kept drifting back.\r\n\r\nTo the sound of his voice.\r\nTo the way he laughed.\r\nTo the ease with which he lived.\r\n\r\nIt was something she didn’t have.\r\n\r\nAnd maybe…\r\nthat was why she couldn’t stop noticing him.\r\n\r\nThe teacher eventually entered, bringing the class back to order. Books opened, pens moved, and the rhythm of learning resumed as if nothing had happened.\r\n\r\nBut for Xu Sui—\r\neverything had already changed.\r\n\r\nShe found herself distracted, her eyes occasionally lifting from the page, drawn to the back of the room without meaning to.\r\n\r\nEach time, she quickly looked away.\r\n\r\nEach time, her heartbeat betrayed her.\r\n\r\nBy the time the final bell rang, the rain had softened into a gentle drizzle. Students began packing up, their energy returning as the day came to an end.\r\n\r\nXu Sui remained seated for a moment longer, staring at the half-finished notes in front of her.\r\n\r\nHer handwriting, usually so neat, had grown uneven.\r\n\r\nShe closed her notebook slowly.\r\n\r\nOutside, the world smelled fresh, washed clean by the rain.\r\n\r\nInside her chest, something unfamiliar had taken root.\r\n\r\nIt was small—\r\nfragile—\r\neasy to ignore.\r\n\r\nBut it was there.\r\n\r\nAnd though she didn’t realize it yet,\r\n\r\nthat quiet, fleeting moment—\r\nthat single glance in a rainy classroom—\r\n\r\nwould stay with her\r\nfor years to come.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-08 13:13:02', '2026-06-21 15:50:15', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(47, 23, 2, 'approved', 'A Feeling She Couldn’t Name', 'The next morning, the sky was clear, as if the storm from yesterday had never happened. Sunlight poured into the classroom, warm and steady, illuminating every corner Xu Sui had always known so well.\r\n\r\nEverything looked the same.\r\n\r\nBut she didn’t feel the same.\r\n\r\nXu Sui arrived early, as usual. She placed her bag neatly on her chair and took out her books, flipping to the page she had marked the night before. Her movements were precise, practiced—almost automatic.\r\n\r\nBut her mind…\r\nwasn’t focused.\r\n\r\nEvery few seconds, she found herself glancing toward the classroom door.\r\n\r\nShe frowned slightly, confused by her own behavior.\r\n\r\nWhat was she expecting?\r\n\r\nThe question lingered—unanswered.\r\n\r\nStudents slowly began to fill the room. Laughter, chatter, footsteps—everything returned to its normal rhythm. Xu Sui lowered her gaze, forcing herself to concentrate on the text in front of her.\r\n\r\nThen—\r\n\r\nA familiar voice cut through the noise.\r\n\r\n“Move, you’re blocking the way.”\r\n\r\nHer hand froze.\r\n\r\nShe didn’t need to look up to know it was him.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze walked in like he always did—unhurried, relaxed, completely unconcerned with the rules that everyone else seemed to follow. His presence brought with it a shift in the atmosphere, subtle but undeniable.\r\n\r\nXu Sui hesitated… then glanced up.\r\n\r\nJust for a second.\r\n\r\nHe passed by her row again, just like yesterday. This time, she noticed more—the faint scent of rain still lingering on his uniform, the way his sleeves were slightly rolled up, the easy confidence in his posture.\r\n\r\nHe didn’t notice her.\r\n\r\nOf course he didn’t.\r\n\r\nWhy would he?\r\n\r\nXu Sui quickly looked down, her fingers tightening slightly around her pen.\r\n\r\nThis is normal, she told herself.\r\n\r\nBut her heart refused to agree.\r\n\r\nThat day, she paid attention to him without meaning to.\r\n\r\nDuring class, he leaned back in his chair, spinning his pen lazily between his fingers. When the teacher asked a question, he either ignored it completely or answered with surprising accuracy—just enough to avoid getting into trouble.\r\n\r\nHe wasn’t careless.\r\n\r\nHe just didn’t care about the same things others did.\r\n\r\nAt lunch, Xu Sui sat with her usual group, quietly eating while her friends chatted about homework and upcoming exams.\r\n\r\n“Did you see Zhou Jingze yesterday?” one of them suddenly said.\r\n\r\nXu Sui’s chopsticks paused mid-air.\r\n\r\n“He came in completely soaked,” another girl added, laughing. “Still acting cool, though.”\r\n\r\n“He always acts like that,” someone else said. “But honestly… he’s kind of attractive.”\r\n\r\n“Kind of?”\r\n\r\n“Okay, very.”\r\n\r\nThe table burst into laughter.\r\n\r\nXu Sui lowered her head, focusing on her food. She didn’t join the conversation.\r\n\r\nBut she listened.\r\n\r\nEvery word stayed with her longer than it should have.\r\n\r\nDays passed.\r\n\r\nThen weeks.\r\n\r\nXu Sui began to notice a pattern—\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze was always surrounded by people, yet somehow distant at the same time.\r\n\r\nHe laughed easily, but never too deeply.\r\nHe talked to everyone, but never stayed too long.\r\n\r\nIt was as if nothing truly mattered to him.\r\n\r\nAnd somehow…\r\nthat made her more curious.\r\n\r\nOne afternoon, as class ended, Xu Sui stayed behind to finish copying notes from the board. Most students had already left, their voices fading down the hallway.\r\n\r\nShe didn’t mind the silence.\r\n\r\nIn fact, she preferred it.\r\n\r\nJust as she was about to close her notebook, a shadow fell across her desk.\r\n\r\nShe looked up—\r\n\r\nAnd her breath caught.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze stood there, looking down at her with a slightly amused expression.\r\n\r\n“You’re still here?” he asked.\r\n\r\nHis voice was closer than she expected.\r\n\r\nXu Sui blinked, caught off guard.\r\n“I… I was just finishing something.”\r\n\r\nHe nodded, glancing at her notebook.\r\n“You always write this much?”\r\n\r\nShe hesitated.\r\n“It helps me remember.”\r\n\r\n“Hm.” He leaned slightly closer, scanning the page. “Looks tiring.”\r\n\r\nShe didn’t know how to respond.\r\n\r\nAfter a moment, he straightened up.\r\n“Well, don’t stay too late. School’s not that important.”\r\n\r\nXu Sui stared at him.\r\n\r\nNot important?\r\n\r\nBefore she could say anything, he turned and walked away, hands in his pockets, as if the conversation had never meant anything.\r\n\r\nThe classroom door closed behind him with a soft click.\r\n\r\nSilence returned.\r\n\r\nBut Xu Sui sat there, unmoving.\r\n\r\nHer heart beat faster than before.\r\n\r\nIt wasn’t a long conversation.\r\nIt wasn’t even meaningful.\r\n\r\nBut to her—\r\n\r\nIt mattered.\r\n\r\nMore than it should have.\r\n\r\nThat night, as she lay in bed, staring at the ceiling, Xu Sui replayed the moment over and over again.\r\n\r\nHis voice.\r\nHis expression.\r\nThe way he stood so close.\r\n\r\nShe turned onto her side, pulling the blanket slightly tighter around herself.\r\n\r\nThis feeling…\r\n\r\nIt was unfamiliar.\r\n\r\nUncomfortable.\r\n\r\nAnd yet—\r\nshe didn’t want it to disappear.\r\n\r\nXu Sui closed her eyes slowly.\r\n\r\nDeep down, she already knew the truth.\r\n\r\nShe just wasn’t ready to admit it yet.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-08 13:14:18', '2026-06-08 13:40:00', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO `chapters` (`id`, `story_id`, `chapter_number`, `chapter_approval_status`, `title`, `content`, `story_progress`, `views`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `rejection_reason`, `is_premium`, `price`, `rejection_highlights`, `is_hidden`) VALUES
(48, 23, 3, 'approved', 'The Distance Between Them', 'Time passed quietly, like pages turning without sound.\r\n\r\nAt first, Xu Sui thought what she felt would fade.\r\n\r\nIt had to.\r\n\r\nFeelings that weren’t meant to be acknowledged usually disappeared on their own—at least, that was what she believed.\r\n\r\nBut this one didn’t.\r\n\r\nIf anything, it grew clearer.\r\n\r\nThe classroom remained the same:\r\nrows of desks, sunlight drifting in through the window, the faint scratching of pens across paper.\r\n\r\nYet Xu Sui found herself distracted more often than before.\r\n\r\nHer eyes would lift without warning—\r\nand land on him.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze.\r\n\r\nHe sat near the back, leaning lazily in his chair, one arm draped over the backrest as if he had no intention of following the rules everyone else obeyed.\r\n\r\nSometimes he listened.\r\nSometimes he didn’t.\r\n\r\nBut even when he seemed uninterested, he always knew what was going on.\r\n\r\nThat was the strange thing about him.\r\n\r\nHe looked careless—\r\nbut he wasn’t.\r\n\r\nOne afternoon, the teacher announced a group assignment.\r\n\r\n“Four people per group. Present next week.”\r\n\r\nChairs shifted immediately as students began forming teams.\r\n\r\nXu Sui lowered her gaze, preparing to quietly join her usual group—\r\n\r\n“Hey.”\r\n\r\nHer hand froze.\r\n\r\nThat voice again.\r\n\r\nShe turned slightly.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze stood beside her desk, looking down at her with a faint, unreadable expression.\r\n\r\n“Join us,” he said.\r\n\r\nUs.\r\n\r\nShe followed his gaze—two other classmates were already waiting nearby.\r\n\r\nFor a moment, Xu Sui hesitated.\r\n\r\nHer first instinct was to refuse.\r\n\r\nBut before she could say anything, one of the group members spoke up,\r\n“Yeah, come on. We need one more person.”\r\n\r\nThere was no good reason to decline.\r\n\r\n“…Okay,” she said softly.\r\n\r\nWorking together felt… strange.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze didn’t act like a leader, nor did he act like someone who didn’t care.\r\n\r\nHe simply did what he wanted.\r\n\r\n“You write,” he told Xu Sui. “You’re good at that.”\r\n\r\nShe blinked.\r\n“How do you know?”\r\n\r\nHe shrugged.\r\n“I’ve seen your notes.”\r\n\r\nHer heart skipped—just for a second.\r\n\r\nHe noticed?\r\n\r\nShe quickly looked down, hiding the flicker of emotion in her eyes.\r\n\r\n“Then… what will you do?”\r\n\r\n“I’ll present.”\r\n\r\nOf course.\r\n\r\nThat suited him perfectly.\r\n\r\nThe days that followed were filled with small interactions.\r\n\r\nNot dramatic.\r\nNot meaningful to anyone else.\r\n\r\nBut to Xu Sui—\r\n\r\nthey mattered.\r\n\r\nThey worked together after class once.\r\n\r\nThe classroom was nearly empty, the sunlight fading into a warm orange glow.\r\n\r\nXu Sui focused on organizing their material, her pen moving steadily across the page.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze sat beside her, unusually quiet.\r\n\r\nAfter a while, he leaned slightly closer.\r\n\r\n“You always write this much?”\r\n\r\nHis voice was low, casual—but close enough that she could feel it.\r\n\r\nXu Sui stiffened slightly.\r\n“It helps me remember.”\r\n\r\n“Seems tiring.”\r\n\r\n“It’s fine.”\r\n\r\nHe didn’t respond immediately.\r\n\r\nFor a moment, there was only silence.\r\n\r\nThen—\r\n\r\n“You’re different from them,” he said suddenly.\r\n\r\nShe paused.\r\n“…What do you mean?”\r\n\r\nHe gestured vaguely.\r\n“Everyone else just does enough to get by.”\r\n\r\n“And me?”\r\n\r\n“You try too hard.”\r\n\r\nXu Sui didn’t know whether that was a compliment or criticism.\r\n\r\n“…That’s just how I am.”\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze looked at her for a moment longer, as if trying to understand something.\r\n\r\nThen he leaned back again.\r\n“Interesting.”\r\n\r\nOn presentation day, everything went smoothly.\r\n\r\nXu Sui handed over the neatly prepared notes.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze stood in front of the class and spoke—\r\n\r\nConfident. Natural. Effortless.\r\n\r\nPeople listened.\r\n\r\nXu Sui sat quietly, watching.\r\n\r\nFor a brief moment, she allowed herself to think—\r\n\r\nIf things were different…\r\n\r\nBut she quickly pushed the thought away.\r\n\r\nThey weren’t.\r\n\r\nAnd they never would be.\r\n\r\nStill— Something had already changed.\r\n\r\nThe distance between them had shortened.\r\n\r\nJust a little.\r\n\r\nBut enough\r\nto make it harder for her to walk away.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-08 13:15:02', '2026-06-08 13:40:01', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(49, 23, 4, 'approved', 'A Line She Shouldn’t Cross', 'Xu Sui believed that feelings, if controlled early enough, could still be undone.\r\n\r\nAfter working with Zhou Jingze on the group assignment, she became more certain of one thing—she was getting too close.\r\n\r\nNot physically.\r\nBut emotionally.\r\n\r\nAnd that was far more dangerous.\r\n\r\nThe next few days, she deliberately changed her routine.\r\n\r\nShe left the classroom immediately after lessons ended.\r\nShe avoided the cafeteria during peak hours.\r\nShe chose quiet corners of the library where no one would find her.\r\n\r\nIt was a quiet retreat.\r\n\r\nA careful step back before things went too far.\r\n\r\nBut Zhou Jingze noticed.\r\n\r\nAt first, it was just a small absence—\r\nshe wasn’t where she usually was.\r\n\r\nThen it became obvious.\r\n\r\nShe was avoiding him.\r\n\r\nOne afternoon, as Xu Sui packed her books quickly after class, a shadow fell across her desk.\r\n\r\nShe didn’t need to look up to know who it was.\r\n\r\n“You’re leaving early again?” Zhou Jingze asked.\r\n\r\nHis tone was casual, but there was something underneath it.\r\n\r\n“…I have things to do,” she replied, keeping her eyes on her bag.\r\n\r\n“You always have things to do.”\r\n\r\nShe paused.\r\n\r\nThen zipped her bag shut.\r\n“That’s normal.”\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze leaned slightly against the desk beside hers.\r\n\r\n“For you, maybe.”\r\n\r\nSilence stretched between them.\r\n\r\nXu Sui didn’t want to look at him.\r\nBecause she knew—\r\nif she did, her resolve might weaken.\r\n\r\n“Are you avoiding me?”\r\n\r\nThe question came suddenly.\r\n\r\nDirect.\r\n\r\nShe froze.\r\n\r\nThen shook her head.\r\n“No.”\r\n\r\n“Then why does it feel like it?”\r\n\r\nShe finally looked up.\r\n\r\nHis gaze was steady—searching, almost.\r\n\r\nFor a brief moment, her heart wavered.\r\n\r\nBut she forced herself to stay calm.\r\n\r\n“You’re overthinking.”\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze held her gaze for a few seconds longer, as if trying to find something hidden beneath her words.\r\n\r\nThen he straightened.\r\n\r\n“Maybe.”\r\n\r\nBut his expression said otherwise.\r\n\r\nThat evening, Xu Sui stayed at the library until it was almost empty.\r\n\r\nThe quiet helped her think.\r\n\r\nShe stared at her open textbook, but her mind drifted elsewhere.\r\n\r\nTo him.\r\n\r\nTo his voice.\r\nTo the way he looked at her earlier.\r\n\r\nShe closed her book slowly.\r\n\r\nThis was exactly what she was afraid of.\r\n\r\nShe wasn’t supposed to care.\r\n\r\nAnd yet—\r\nshe did.\r\n\r\nWhen she finally stepped outside, the campus was dimly lit.\r\n\r\nCool air brushed against her skin as she walked down the steps.\r\n\r\nThen—\r\n\r\n“Xu Sui.”\r\n\r\nHer steps stopped.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze stood a few meters away, hands in his pockets.\r\n\r\n“You’re still here?” she asked, surprised.\r\n\r\n“I could ask you the same thing.”\r\n\r\n“…I had work.”\r\n\r\nHe nodded, then walked closer.\r\n\r\nThe distance between them shrank—slowly, deliberately.\r\n\r\n“You didn’t answer me earlier.”\r\n\r\n“…About what?”\r\n\r\n“Why you’re avoiding me.”\r\n\r\nXu Sui looked away.\r\n\r\n“I told you—I’m not.”\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze studied her quietly.\r\n\r\nThen said, softer this time,\r\n\r\n“If it doesn’t mean anything… why does it feel like it does?”\r\n\r\nHer breath caught.\r\n\r\nFor a second—just a second—\r\nshe wanted to tell the truth.\r\n\r\nBut she didn’t.\r\n\r\nBecause crossing that line…\r\n\r\nwould change everything.\r\n\r\n“…You’re misunderstanding,” she said finally.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze didn’t reply.\r\n\r\nHe simply watched her.\r\n\r\nThen stepped back.\r\n\r\n“Alright.”\r\n\r\nBut this time, neither of them believed it.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-08 13:18:19', '2026-06-08 13:40:03', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(50, 23, 5, 'approved', 'The Confession 💔', 'The party was louder than Xu Sui expected.\r\n\r\nMusic pulsed through the room, conversations overlapping into a chaotic rhythm of laughter and voices. Lights flickered softly, casting shifting shadows across unfamiliar faces.\r\n\r\nShe stood near the edge, her fingers lightly gripping a glass she hadn’t touched.\r\n\r\nShe didn’t belong here.\r\n\r\nBut he did.\r\n\r\nZhou Jingze stood across the room, surrounded by people as always.\r\n\r\nHe was laughing—easy, effortless, like nothing in the world could trouble him.\r\n\r\nXu Sui watched him quietly.\r\n\r\nThere was a distance between them.\r\n\r\nNot physical—\r\nbut real.\r\n\r\nShe had tried to step away.\r\n\r\nTried to convince herself it didn’t matter.\r\n\r\nBut the truth was—\r\nit did.\r\n\r\nMore than she wanted to admit.\r\n\r\nMaybe that was why she walked toward him.\r\n\r\nBefore she could think.\r\nBefore she could stop herself.\r\n\r\nStep by step—\r\n\r\nUntil she stood right in front of him.\r\n\r\n“Zhou Jingze.”\r\n\r\nHe turned, surprised.\r\n“Hey—”\r\n\r\n“I like you.”\r\n\r\nThe words came out clearly.\r\n\r\nNo hesitation.\r\nNo escape.\r\n\r\nJust truth.\r\n\r\nThe noise around them seemed to fade.\r\n\r\nFor a moment, Zhou Jingze didn’t speak.\r\n\r\nHis expression shifted—subtle, but enough.\r\n\r\nThen he exhaled slowly.\r\n\r\n“You shouldn’t.”\r\n\r\nXu Sui felt something tighten inside her chest.\r\n\r\n“…Why?”\r\n\r\n“Because you’re not like the others,” he said. “You’re serious. Careful. You think too much.”\r\n\r\n“And that’s bad?”\r\n\r\n“For me, yes.”\r\n\r\nThe answer was calm.\r\n\r\nHonest.\r\n\r\nAnd painful.\r\n\r\n“I don’t want to hurt you,” he added.\r\n\r\nXu Sui almost laughed.\r\n\r\nToo late.\r\n\r\nBut she didn’t say that.\r\n\r\nInstead, she nodded slowly.\r\n\r\n“I understand.”\r\n\r\nHer voice didn’t break.\r\n\r\nHer expression didn’t change.\r\n\r\nBut inside—\r\n\r\nsomething quietly collapsed.\r\n\r\n“I’m sorry,” Zhou Jingze said again.\r\n\r\nShe smiled faintly.\r\n“It’s okay.”\r\n\r\nAnd just like that—\r\n\r\nit ended.\r\n\r\nNo drama.\r\nNo tears.\r\n\r\nJust a simple rejection…\r\nthat stayed with her far longer than it should have.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-08 13:19:03', '2026-06-08 13:40:02', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(51, 23, 6, 'approved', 'Learning to Let Go', 'Heartbreak didn’t arrive all at once.\r\n\r\nIt settled slowly—quietly—like something sinking deep beneath the surface where no one could see it.\r\n\r\nXu Sui didn’t cry in public.\r\n\r\nShe didn’t talk about it.\r\n\r\nShe simply continued her life—\r\nas if nothing had happened.\r\n\r\nBut she changed.\r\n\r\nSubtly.\r\n\r\nShe stopped attending gatherings.\r\nStopped lingering in places where she might see him.\r\nStopped allowing her thoughts to wander.\r\n\r\nAt first, it was difficult.\r\n\r\nEvery familiar place reminded her of something—\r\na conversation, a glance, a moment that once meant more than it should have.\r\n\r\nBut over time, those memories lost their sharp edges.\r\n\r\nOne evening, she sat alone in the library, surrounded by silence.\r\n\r\nHer textbook lay open in front of her, but she wasn’t reading.\r\n\r\nInstead, she stared at the page—unfocused.\r\n\r\n“Xu Sui?”\r\n\r\nShe looked up, startled.\r\n\r\nA classmate stood nearby, smiling.\r\n\r\n“You’ve been here all day. Aren’t you tired?”\r\n\r\nShe shook her head.\r\n“I’m fine.”\r\n\r\nAnd she was.\r\n\r\nOr at least—\r\nshe was learning to be.\r\n\r\nLetting go wasn’t about forgetting.\r\n\r\nIt was about accepting.\r\n\r\nAccepting that some feelings didn’t lead anywhere.\r\nAccepting that not everything had a happy beginning.\r\n\r\nXu Sui closed her book slowly.\r\n\r\nOutside, the sky had already darkened.\r\n\r\nShe stood, gathering her things with calm, steady movements.\r\n\r\nFor the first time in a long while—\r\n\r\nshe felt… lighter.\r\n\r\nBut what she didn’t know was—\r\n\r\nwhile she was learning to let go,\r\n\r\nsomeone else…\r\n\r\nwas only just beginning to understand.', 'published', 0, '2026-06-08 13:19:25', '2026-06-08 13:41:16', NULL, 1, 0.25, NULL, 0),
(52, 24, 1, 'approved', 'ការចាប់ផ្តើមថ្មី', 'ស្រីនាងគឺជានិស្សិតឆ្នាំទី៣ នៅសាកលវិទ្យាល័យមួយនៅភ្នំពេញ។ នាងមានជីវិតធម្មតា ប៉ុន្តែចូលចិត្តសរសេររឿង និងស្រមៃពីអនាគត។ ថ្ងៃមួយ នាងបានជួបកំលោះម្នាក់ឈ្មោះដារា នៅបណ្ណាល័យ។\r\nដារាជាមនុស្សស្ងប់ស្ងាត់ ប៉ុន្តែភ្នែករបស់គាត់ពោរពេញដោយអាថ៌កំបាំង។ ការជួបគ្នានោះបានធ្វើឲ្យជីវិតស្រីនាងផ្លាស់ប្តូរ។', 'published', 0, '2026-06-14 08:35:25', '2026-06-14 09:28:10', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(53, 24, 2, 'approved', 'មិត្តភាពក្លាយជាស្នេហា', 'ស្រីនាងនិងដារា ចាប់ផ្តើមស្គាល់គ្នាបានកាន់តែច្រើន។ ពួកគេចូលចិត្តអង្គុយនិយាយក្រោមដើមឈើធំក្នុងសាលា។\r\nថ្ងៃមួយ ដារាបានប្រាប់ថា៖\r\n“បើមានថ្ងៃណាខ្ញុំបាត់ទៅ អ្នកនឹងចាំខ្ញុំទេ?”\r\nស្រីនាងញញឹមហើយនិយាយថា៖ “ខ្ញុំមិនអោយអ្នកបាត់ទៅទេ”\r\nពាក្យនោះបានក្លាយជាការចាប់ផ្តើមនៃស្នេហា។', 'published', 0, '2026-06-14 08:39:03', '2026-06-16 04:23:41', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(54, 24, 3, 'approved', 'អាថ៌កំបាំងដែលលាក់លៀម', 'ដារាមានអាកប្បកិរិយាខុសពីមុន។ គាត់មិនឆ្លើយសាររបស់ស្រីនាងជាញឹកញាប់ទេ។\r\nស្រីនាងចាប់ផ្តើមសង្ស័យ។ នៅថ្ងៃមួយ នាងបានឃើញដារានៅមន្ទីរពេទ្យ។\r\nគាត់កំពុងព្យាបាលជំងឺធ្ងន់មួយដែលគាត់មិនធ្លាប់ប្រាប់នាង។', 'published', 0, '2026-06-14 08:39:15', '2026-06-16 11:09:02', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(55, 24, 4, 'approved', 'សេចក្ដីពិត', 'ដារាប្រាប់ស្រីនាងថា គាត់មានជំងឺបេះដូងដែលអាចគ្រោះថ្នាក់។\r\n“ខ្ញុំមិនចង់អោយអ្នកឈឺចាប់ទេ” គាត់និយាយ\r\nស្រីនាងទទូចថា៖ “ស្នេហាមិនមែនសម្រាប់ថ្ងៃងាយៗទេ តែសម្រាប់ថ្ងៃពិបាកផងដែរ”\r\nពួកគេចាប់ដៃគ្នា ដើម្បីប្រឈមមុខជាមួយអនាគត។ អនាគតជាអ្វី?', 'published', 0, '2026-06-14 08:39:56', '2026-06-21 19:50:36', NULL, 0, 0.00, '[{\"text\": \"ដារាប្រាប់ស្រីនាងថា គាត់មានជំងឺបេះដូងដែលអាចគ្រោះថ្នាក់។\", \"comment\": \"look a bit wierd\"}]', 0),
(56, 24, 5, 'approved', 'ពេលវេលាដែលមានតម្លៃ', 'ស្រីនាង និងដារា ចំណាយពេលជាមួយគ្នាឲ្យបានច្រើន។\r\nពួកគេទៅមើលថ្ងៃលិច ទៅញ៉ាំអាហារ និងសរសេរចំណងជើងស្នេហារួមគ្នា។\r\nទោះបីមានភាពភ័យខ្លាច ក៏ពួកគេនៅតែជឿលើស្នេហា។ តើអ្វីជាពេលវេលាទៅ? អនាគតជាអ្វី?', 'published', 0, '2026-06-14 08:40:09', '2026-06-21 20:18:47', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(58, 24, 6, 'pending', 'ថ្ងៃដែលមិនអាចជៀសវាង', 'សុខភាពដារាធ្លាក់ចុះយ៉ាងខ្លាំង។ គាត់ត្រូវចូលមន្ទីរពេទ្យជាញឹកញាប់។\r\nស្រីនាងនៅក្បែរគាត់រាល់ថ្ងៃ។\r\nយប់មួយ ដារាបាននិយាយថា៖\r\n“បើថ្ងៃណាខ្ញុំមិននៅ អ្នកត្រូវរស់បន្ត ហើយកុំភ្លេចសុបិនរបស់អ្នក”\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងទ្រាំទឹកភ្នែកមិនបាន។ នាងចាប់ដៃគាត់យ៉ាងរឹងមាំ ហើយនិយាយថា៖\r\n“អ្នកគឺជាសុបិនរបស់ខ្ញុំ”\r\n\r\nថ្ងៃបន្ទាប់ គ្រូពេទ្យបានប្រាប់ថាស្ថានភាពគាត់គ្រោះថ្នាក់។\r\nស្រីនាងនៅក្រៅបន្ទប់ ICU មិនចាកចេញទេ។\r\nនាងចាប់ផ្តើមសរសេររឿងអំពីពួកគេ ដើម្បីរក្សាអនុស្សាវរីយ៍។', 'draft', 0, '2026-06-14 09:10:28', '2026-06-21 12:51:23', NULL, 0, 0.00, NULL, 0),
(59, 24, 7, 'approved', 'ពន្លឺក្រោមព្រះច័ន្ទ', 'យប់មួយក្រោមពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទ ស្រីនាងអង្គុយស្ងៀមស្ងាត់នៅលើកៅអីតូចមុខមន្ទីរពេទ្យ។ ខ្យល់ត្រជាក់បន្តិចបក់តាមស្លឹកឈើ បង្កើតសំឡេងស្រាលៗដូចជាបទភ្លេងដ៏ស្ងប់ស្ងាត់មួយ។ ពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទភ្លឺចាំងលើដីស្ងួត បង្កើតស្រមោលវែងៗដែលមើលទៅឯកោដូចអារម្មណ៍ក្នុងចិត្តនាង។\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងអង្គុយបង្វិលម្រាមដៃខ្លួនឯង ដោយមិនដឹងថាគួរធ្វើអ្វីទៀត។ ភ្នែកនាងមើលទៅទិសឆ្ងាយ ប៉ុន្តែមិនបានមើលឃើញអ្វីជាក់លាក់ទេ។ អ្វីដែលនាងឃើញ គឺអតីតកាល—អនុស្សាវរីយ៍ដែលមិនអាចត្រឡប់វិញបាន។\r\n\r\nនាងចងចាំថ្ងៃដំបូងដែលបានជួបដារា។ នៅក្នុងបណ្ណាល័យដែលស្ងប់ស្ងាត់ មានតែសំឡេងបើកទំព័រសៀវភៅ។ ដារាអង្គុយនៅជ្រុងមួយ កំពុងអានសៀវភៅយ៉ាងផ្តោតអារម្មណ៍។ ពេលនាងដើរចូលទៅ គាត់បានលើកភ្នែកឡើង ហើយញញឹមតិចតួច—ស្នាមញញឹមដែលបានបង្កើតការចាប់ផ្តើមមួយដែលនាងមិនដែលរំពឹងទុក។\r\n\r\n“អ្នកចូលចិត្តអានប្រភេទនេះដែរ?” គាត់បានសួរយ៉ាងសាមញ្ញ។\r\nនាងក៏ឆ្លើយដោយការស្ទាក់ស្ទើរ តែមានស្នាមញញឹម។\r\nពីពេលនោះ ការសន្ទនាតូចៗ បានក្លាយជាការស្គាល់គ្នា។ ការស្គាល់គ្នា បានក្លាយជាមិត្តភាព។ ហើយមិត្តភាពបានក្លាយជាស្នេហា។\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងដកដង្ហើមយឺតៗ ហើយយកសៀវភៅកំណត់ហេតុចេញពីកាបូប។ សៀវភៅតូចនេះគឺជាអ្វីដែលនាងសរសេររាល់អារម្មណ៍របស់នាង។ នាងបើកទៅទំព័រមួយ ដែលមានអក្សរដែលនាងបានសរសេរនៅថ្ងៃដែលដារាប្រាប់ពីជំងឺរបស់គាត់។\r\n\r\n“ថ្ងៃនេះ ខ្ញុំបានដឹងថាពេលវេលារបស់យើងមិនដូចអ្នកដទៃ… តែខ្ញុំមិនចង់បោះបង់ទេ…”\r\n\r\nទឹកភ្នែកធ្លាក់ចុះលើទំព័រសៀវភៅ បង្កើតស្នាមសើមតូចៗ។ នាងបិទសៀវភៅយឺតៗ ហើយចាប់វាជិតបេះដូង។\r\n\r\nភ្លាមៗ ទ្វារបន្ទប់ ICU បើកឡើង។ សំឡេងនោះដូចជាការដាក់សញ្ញាថាពេលវេលាសំខាន់មួយកំពុងមកដល់។\r\n\r\nគ្រូពេទ្យដើរចេញមក ដោយមុខស្ងប់ស្ងាត់ ប៉ុន្តែមិនអាចលាក់អារម្មណ៍បានទេ។\r\n\r\n“គាត់…?” ស្រីនាងសួរដោយសម្លេងញ័រ។\r\n\r\nគ្រូពេទ្យស្ងៀមបន្តិច មុននឹងនិយាយ៖\r\n“សូមទោស… យើងបានព្យាយាមអស់ពីសមត្ថភាពហើយ…”\r\n\r\nពេលនោះពិភពលោកដូចជាឈប់ដំណើរការ។ សំឡេងគ្រប់យ៉ាងបាត់បង់។ មានតែភាពស្ងៀមស្ងាត់ និងការឈឺចាប់ដ៏ធំមួយនៅក្នុងបេះដូង។\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងមិនដឹងថានាងដើរចូលទៅក្នុងបន្ទប់យ៉ាងដូចម្តេចទេ។ ជំហាននាងធ្ងន់ដូចមានអ្វីមួយទាញចុះ។\r\n\r\nដារាគេងស្ងៀមនៅលើគ្រែ។ មុខរបស់គាត់ស្ងប់ស្ងាត់ ដូចជាមនុស្សដែលកំពុងដេកលក់។ ស្នាមញញឹមតិចតួចនៅលើបបូរមាត់គាត់ នៅតែដូចដើម។\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងអង្គុយជិតគាត់ ហើយកាន់ដៃគាត់យ៉ាងប្រុងប្រយ័ត្ន។\r\nដៃនោះត្រជាក់… ប៉ុន្តែសម្រាប់នាង វានៅតែជាដៃដែលធ្លាប់ផ្តល់កំលាំង។\r\n\r\n“អ្នកបានប្រាប់ឲ្យខ្ញុំរស់បន្ត…” នាងនិយាយយឺតៗ\r\n“ខ្ញុំនឹងព្យាយាម… ទោះបីខ្ញុំខ្សោយប៉ុណ្ណាក៏ដោយ…”\r\n\r\nនាងដាក់មុខលើដៃគាត់ ហើយយំស្ងៀមៗ។ មិនមានសំឡេងឡើយ មានតែអារម្មណ៍ដែលពោរពេញដោយការបាត់បង់។\r\n\r\nខ្យល់ត្រជាក់បក់ចូលតាមបង្អួច។ ពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទចូលមកក្នុងបន្ទប់ បំភ្លឺលើគាត់ និងនាង។ នៅក្នុងពន្លឺនោះ នាងមានអារម្មណ៍ដូចជាគាត់នៅតែជាមួយនាង។\r\n\r\nថ្ងៃបន្ទាប់ៗមក ជីវិតស្រីនាងដូចជាក្លាយជាពណ៌ប្រផេះ។\r\nនាងត្រឡប់ទៅសាលា ប៉ុន្តែអ្វីៗមើលទៅមិនដូចមុន។\r\nកៅអីដែលពួកគេធ្លាប់អង្គុយ នៅតែស្ថិតនៅទីនោះ ប៉ុន្តែគ្មានគាត់។\r\n\r\nមិត្តរបស់នាងព្យាយាមលួងលោម ប៉ុន្តែគ្មាននរណាអាចយល់អារម្មណ៍នាងពិតៗបានទេ។\r\n\r\nយប់មួយ នាងត្រឡប់ទៅកន្លែងដែលពួកគេចូលចិត្ត—មាត់បឹងដែលពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទភ្លឺលើផ្ទៃទឹក។\r\n\r\nនាងអង្គុយនៅកន្លែងដើម ហើយមើលទៅលើមេឃ។\r\n\r\n“អ្នកនៅទីនេះទេ…?” នាងនិយាយស្រាលៗ\r\n\r\nខ្យល់បក់តិចៗ ប៉ះលើសក់នាង ដូចជាការឆ្លើយតបមួយ។\r\n\r\nនាងញញឹមបន្តិច ទោះបីភ្នែកនៅតែមានទឹកភ្នែក។\r\n\r\nនៅថ្ងៃនោះ នាងចាប់ផ្តើមសរសេររឿងយ៉ាងពិតប្រាកដ។ មិនមែនជារឿងស្រមៃទេ ប៉ុន្តែជារឿងជីវិតរបស់នាង និងដារា។\r\n\r\nពាក្យនីមួយៗ ក្លាយជាវិធីមួយក្នុងការរក្សាគាត់នៅជាមួយនាង។\r\n\r\nខែបន្ទាប់ៗមក សៀវភៅនោះចាប់ផ្តើមរួចរាល់។\r\nនាងដាក់ចំណងជើងថា “ពន្លឺក្រោមព្រះច័ន្ទ” — ពន្លឺដែលតំណាងឲ្យស្នេហារបស់ពួកគេ។\r\n\r\nឆ្នាំមួយក្រោយ សៀវភៅនោះត្រូវបានបោះពុម្ព។ មនុស្សជាច្រើនបានអាន និងមានអារម្មណ៍រំភើបចិត្ត។\r\n\r\nនៅថ្ងៃបង្ហាញសៀវភៅ នាងឈរលើឆាក ដោយដៃញ័រតិចៗ។\r\n\r\n“រឿងនេះ…” នាងនិយាយ\r\n“គឺជារឿងអំពីមនុស្សម្នាក់ ដែលបានបង្រៀនខ្ញុំអំពីស្នេហា និងការរស់បន្ត…”\r\n\r\nនាងមើលឡើងទៅលើមេឃ តាមបង្អួចកញ្ចក់។ ព្រះច័ន្ទនៅទីនោះ ដូចជាដើម។\r\n\r\nយប់នោះ នាងឈរម្នាក់ឯងក្រោមពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទ។\r\n\r\n“ខ្ញុំកំពុងរស់បន្តហើយ…” នាងនិយាយស្រាលៗ\r\n“ហើយខ្ញុំនឹងរស់ឲ្យល្អ សម្រាប់អ្នក…”\r\n\r\nខ្យល់បក់ស្រាលៗ ហើយនាងមានអារម្មណ៍ដូចជាមាននរណាម្នាក់នៅក្បែរនាង។\r\n\r\nពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទនៅតែភ្លឺ—មិនធ្លាប់ផុតទៅណាទេ។ ដូចជាស្នេហា ដែលមិនដែលបាត់បង់ពិតៗ ប៉ុន្តែរស់នៅក្នុងការចងចាំ។\r\n\r\nនិងនៅក្នុងបេះដូងរបស់ស្រីនាង ដារាមិនដែលបាត់បង់ឡើយ។ គាត់គ្រាន់តែបំលែងទៅជាពន្លឺមួយ—ពន្លឺក្រោមព្រះច័ន្ទ ដែលនឹងបន្តភ្លឺជាមួយនាងជានិច្ច។\r\n\r\nពេលរាត្រីកាន់តែជ្រៅ ស្រីនាងនៅតែឈរនៅទីនោះ មិនចង់ចាកចេញពីពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទឡើយ។ នាងមានអារម្មណ៍ថា ប្រសិនបើនាងដើរចេញពីទីនេះ នាងនឹងបាត់បង់អ្វីមួយដែលនៅសល់ចុងក្រោយពីដារា។\r\n\r\nនាងអង្គុយចុះវិញ ហើយយកសៀវភៅកំណត់ហេតុចេញម្តងទៀត។ នៅចុងក្រោយបំផុត មានទំព័រទទេមួយដែលនាងមិនធ្លាប់សរសេរឡើយ។\r\n\r\nនាងកាន់ប៊ិច ហើយស្ទាក់ស្ទើរបន្តិច មុននឹងសរសេរ៖\r\n\r\n“យប់នេះ ខ្ញុំមកទីនេះម្នាក់ឯង ប៉ុន្តែខ្ញុំមិនមានអារម្មណ៍ថាខ្ញុំឯកោទេ។ ខ្ញុំមានអារម្មណ៍ថា អ្នកនៅក្បែរខ្ញុំ ដូចថ្ងៃដើមៗ…”\r\n\r\nបន្ទាប់មក នាងឈប់សរសេរ ហើយញញឹមតិចតួច។ ទោះបីជាទឹកភ្នែកនៅតែមាន ក៏ស្នាមញញឹមនោះមិនមែនជាស្នាមញញឹមនៃការឈឺចាប់ទេ ប៉ុន្តែជាស្នាមញញឹមនៃការយល់ព្រមទទួល។\r\n\r\nនាងចងចាំពាក្យចុងក្រោយដែលដារាបាននិយាយជាមួយនាង—ពាក្យដែលធ្វើឲ្យនាងឈឺចាប់បំផុត ប៉ុន្តែផ្តល់កម្លាំងបំផុតផងដែរ។\r\n\r\n“រស់បន្ត…”\r\n\r\nពាក្យសាមញ្ញប៉ុន្តែមានន័យធំ។\r\n\r\nនៅថ្ងៃបន្ទាប់ ស្រីនាងចាប់ផ្តើមផ្លាស់ប្តូរជីវិតរបស់នាងបន្តិចម្តងៗ។ នាងមិននៅតែស្ថិតក្នុងអតីតកាលទៀតទេ ប៉ុន្តែនាងយកអតីតកាលមកជាកម្លាំង។\r\n\r\nនាងចូលរៀនវិញ ដោយព្យាយាមផ្តោតលើការសិក្សា។ ទោះបីពេលខ្លះនាងនៅតែគិតដល់ដារា ហើយភ្នែកនាងស្រកទឹកភ្នែក ក៏នាងមិនគេចចេញពីអារម្មណ៍ទៀតទេ។ នាងរៀនទទួលយកវា។\r\n\r\nមិត្តរបស់នាងក៏ចាប់ផ្តើមឃើញការផ្លាស់ប្តូរ។ ពួកគេឃើញថា ស្រីនាងមិនមែនត្រឹមតែសោកស្តាយទេ ប៉ុន្តែនាងកំពុងរឹងមាំឡើង។\r\n\r\nថ្ងៃមួយ គ្រូបង្រៀនបានសួរនាង៖\r\n“តើអ្វីជាអ្វីដែលជំរុញឲ្យអ្នកសរសេររឿងនេះ?”\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងស្ងៀមបន្តិច មុននឹងឆ្លើយ៖\r\n“មនុស្សម្នាក់… ដែលខ្ញុំមិនអាចភ្លេចបាន”\r\n\r\nសំឡេងនាងទន់ ប៉ុន្តែពោរពេញដោយអារម្មណ៍។\r\n\r\nពេលរាត្រីមកដល់ នាងនៅតែមានទម្លាប់មើលព្រះច័ន្ទ។ តែខុសពីមុន នាងមិនយំជានិច្ចទៀតទេ។ ពេលខ្លះ នាងគ្រាន់តែអង្គុយស្ងៀមៗ ហើយមានអារម្មណ៍សុខសាន្តបន្តិច។\r\n\r\nនាងចាប់ផ្តើមយល់ថា ស្នេហាមិនមែនបាត់បង់ទេ—វាគ្រាន់តែប្តូរទម្រង់។ ពីការមានគ្នា ទៅជាការចងចាំ។ ពីការប៉ះបាន ទៅជាការរស់នៅក្នុងបេះដូង។\r\n\r\nឆ្នាំបន្ទាប់ៗមក ស្រីនាងក្លាយជាអ្នកនិពន្ធដែលមានអ្នកអានច្រើន។ រឿងរបស់នាងមិនមែនត្រឹមតែជារឿងស្នេហាទេ ប៉ុន្តែជារឿងដែលប៉ះពាល់ដល់បេះដូងមនុស្សជាច្រើន។\r\n\r\nមនុស្សជាច្រើនបានសរសេរសារមកនាង ប្រាប់ថា រឿងរបស់នាងបានជួយពួកគេឲ្យឆ្លងកាត់ការបាត់បង់ដូចគ្នា។\r\n\r\nស្រីនាងអានសារទាំងនោះ ហើយញញឹម។ នាងដឹងថា ដារាមិនត្រឹមតែផ្លាស់ប្តូរជីវិតនាងទេ ប៉ុន្តែតាមរយៈនាង គាត់ក៏បានប៉ះពាល់ដល់ជីវិតអ្នកដទៃផងដែរ។\r\n\r\nយប់មួយក្រោមពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទ នាងត្រឡប់ទៅកន្លែងចាស់ម្តងទៀត។\r\n\r\nនាងអង្គុយនៅលើកៅអីដដែល ហើយមើលទៅលើមេឃ។\r\n\r\n“អ្នកឃើញទេ…?” នាងនិយាយស្រាលៗ\r\n“ខ្ញុំបានធ្វើបានហើយ…”\r\n\r\nខ្យល់បក់ស្រាលៗ ប៉ះលើមុខនាង ដូចជាការឆ្លើយតបមួយដែលពោរពេញដោយភាពកក់ក្តៅ។\r\n\r\nនាងបិទភ្នែកបន្តិច ហើយដកដង្ហើមយឺតៗ។ នៅក្នុងចិត្តនាង មានភាពស្ងប់ស្ងាត់ដែលនាងមិនធ្លាប់មានពីមុន។\r\n\r\nពេលនាងបើកភ្នែកវិញ ពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទនៅតែភ្លឺដូចដើម។\r\n\r\nប៉ុន្តេលើកនេះ នាងមិនមើលវាជាសញ្ញានៃការបាត់បង់ទៀតទេ—នាងមើលវាជាសញ្ញានៃសេចក្ដីស្រឡាញ់ដែលនៅតែបន្ត។\r\n\r\nនាងញញឹម ហើយឈរឡើងយឺតៗ។\r\n\r\nជំហាននាងមិនធ្ងន់ដូចមុនទៀតទេ។ នាងដើរចេញពីកន្លែងនោះ ដោយមានអារម្មណ៍ថា នាងមិនមែនដើរតែម្នាក់ឯងទេ។\r\n\r\nនៅពីក្រោយនាង ពន្លឺព្រះច័ន្ទនៅតែភ្លឺ—ដូចជាកំពុងថែរក្សា អនុស្សាវរីយ៍ដែលមិនដែលរលាយបាត់។\r\n\r\nហើយនៅក្នុងបេះដូងស្រីនាង ស្នេហានោះនៅតែរស់—មិនមែនជាការឈឺចាប់ទៀតទេ ប៉ុន្តែជាកម្លាំងមួយ ដែលជំរុញឲ្យនាងរស់បន្ត និងស្រឡាញ់ជីវិត។', 'published', 0, '2026-06-14 09:17:23', '2026-06-21 20:22:05', NULL, 1, 0.25, NULL, 0);

